var e={"eye-ent-red-eye":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A red eye is dilatation of **conjunctival, episcleral or ciliary vessels**.`,`The layer of vessels that is dilated names the diagnosis.`,`About **80% of red eyes** in general practice are conjunctivitis.`,`Conjunctivitis is a **diagnosis of exclusion** once the eye is painful or vision falls.`,`Red eye with normal vision and no true pain is treated in the OPD.`,`Red eye with **reduced vision, pain or photophobia** is referred the same day.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Conjunctival injection** is brightest in the fornices, moves and blanches: surface disease.`,`**Ciliary injection** is a deep flush at the limbus: cornea, iris or ciliary body.`,`Conjunctivitis is classified by cause: bacterial, viral, chlamydial, allergic and toxic.`,`**Ophthalmia neonatorum** is any conjunctivitis in the first 28 days of life.`,`Uveitis is graded anatomically by **SUN**; primary care mainly sees acute anterior uveitis.`,`India was validated by WHO as **trachoma-free in 2024**; trichiasis surveillance continues.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Surface inflammation dilates conjunctival vessels, causing grittiness and discharge.`,`2. Loss of corneal epithelium exposes nerves, so pain is severe and **fluorescein stains**.`,`3. Inflamed iris and ciliary muscle hurt when light makes them contract: **photophobia**.`,`4. In uveitis, protein and cells leak into aqueous as **flare and cells**.`,`5. In angle closure, **pupillary block** pushes the iris forward and closes the angle.`,`6. IOP rises to **40-80 mmHg**, causing corneal oedema, a fixed pupil and vomiting.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask five questions: **blurred vision**, true pain, discharge type, photophobia, one or both eyes.`,`Itch means **allergy**; infection does not itch.`,`Purulent discharge with lids stuck on waking suggests bacterial conjunctivitis.`,`Watery discharge with a cold and a tender node suggests **adenovirus**.`,`Ask about **contact lens wear** and trauma with vegetable matter.`,`Ask about **over-the-counter steroid drops** and home remedies already used.`,`Haloes, brow ache and vomiting in an older long-sighted patient suggest **angle closure**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check **visual acuity first**, each eye separately, with a pinhole if below 6/6.`,`Do the **oblique torch shadow test** for a shallow chamber before dilating.`,`Evert the upper lid in any foreign-body sensation.`,`**Fluorescein** with cobalt-blue light: a branching dendrite with terminal bulbs means herpes simplex.`,`Pupil small and irregular suggests uveitis; mid-dilated, oval and fixed suggests angle closure.`,`Digital tension: a **stony hard eye** suggests angle closure.`,`**Phenylephrine 2.5%** blanches episcleritis but not scleritis.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Simple conjunctivitis needs no test**; the diagnosis is clinical.`,`Swab neonates for Gram stain, culture and chlamydia NAAT.`,`**Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** mean gonococcus; treat systemically the same day.`,`The ophthalmologist takes a **corneal scraping with KOH** before any drop in an ulcer.`,`Tonometry in angle closure typically reads **over 40 mmHg**.`,`Recurrent uveitis: HLA-B27, chest X-ray, IGRA and VDRL as history suggests.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Bacterial: **chloramphenicol 0.5%** two-hourly for 2 days, then four times daily for 5 days.`,`Contact lens wearers get **moxifloxacin 0.5%** three times daily for 7 days.`,`Adenovirus: preservative-free lubricants and cold compresses; antibiotics do not help.`,`Allergic: **olopatadine 0.1%** twice daily; start cromoglicate before the season.`,`Hygiene: wash hands, separate towels, no shared drops; adenovirus is contagious **10-14 days**.`,`**Never start steroid drops** in an undiagnosed red eye.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Gonococcal: **ceftriaxone 1 g IM** single dose in adults.`,`Neonatal gonococcal: ceftriaxone 25-50 mg/kg, maximum 125 mg, plus hourly saline lavage.`,`Chlamydial: azithromycin 1 g adult; neonate oral erythromycin for 14 days.`,`Herpes simplex keratitis: **acyclovir 3% ointment** five times daily for 14 days.`,`Uveitis (ophthalmologist): prednisolone acetate 1% with a cycloplegic, tapered over 4-6 weeks.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Gonococcus can **perforate the cornea** within 24-48 hours.`,`Steroid drops cause **raised IOP**, cataract and spread of herpes or fungal ulcers.`,`A painful red eye in a lens wearer is **Pseudomonas or Acanthamoeba** until proved otherwise.`,`Hypopyon, hyphaema or loss of red reflex needs immediate referral.`,`Red painful eye within 6 weeks of surgery is **endophthalmitis**.`,`The fellow eye in angle closure has a **40-80% risk** without laser iridotomy.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer **within hours**: angle closure, chemical injury after irrigation, endophthalmitis, penetrating injury.`,`Chemical splash: irrigate with 1-2 litres for **at least 30 minutes** before anything else.`,`Angle closure first aid: **acetazolamide 500 mg**, timolol, brimonidine, then pilocarpine once IOP falls.`,`Review bacterial conjunctivitis at **5-7 days** and adenoviral at 2-3 weeks.`,`Anyone on topical steroid over 2 weeks needs an **IOP check**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Five causes of red eye at the bedside`,columns:[`Feature`,`Conjunctivitis`,`Keratitis`,`Anterior uveitis`,`Angle closure`],rows:[[`Vision`,`Normal`,`Reduced`,`Mildly reduced`,`Markedly reduced, haloes`],[`Pain`,`Gritty only`,`Severe`,`Aching, photophobia`,`Severe, with vomiting`],[`Injection`,`Conjunctival`,`Ciliary`,`Ciliary`,`Mixed`],[`Cornea`,`Clear`,`Infiltrate, stains`,`Keratic precipitates`,`Hazy, oedematous`],[`Pupil`,`Normal`,`Normal or small`,`Small, irregular`,`Mid-dilated, oval, fixed`],[`Tension`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal or low`,`Stony hard`],[`Action`,`Treat in OPD`,`Refer same day`,`Refer same day`,`Treat, refer within hours`]]},{heading:`Drugs in red eye`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Chloramphenicol 0.5% drops`,`1 drop 2-hourly x 2 days, then QID`,`5 days; 1% ointment at night`,`Cheap first line; stinging`],[`Moxifloxacin 0.5% drops`,`1 drop three times daily`,`7 days`,`Keep for lens wearers, severe cases`],[`Olopatadine 0.1% drops`,`1 drop twice daily`,`Through the season`,`Brief stinging, headache`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1 g IM once (neonate 25-50 mg/kg, max 125 mg)`,`Single dose`,`Avoid in jaundiced or premature neonates`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g oral once (trachoma 20 mg/kg)`,`Single dose`,`Treat sexual partners in chlamydia`],[`Acyclovir 3% ointment`,`5 times daily`,`14 days`,`Punctate keratopathy; no steroid`],[`Prednisolone acetate 1% drops`,`1 drop 1-2 hourly (uveitis)`,`Taper over 4-6 weeks`,`Raised IOP, cataract; ophthalmologist only`],[`Acetazolamide`,`500 mg IV/oral, then 250 mg 6-hourly`,`Acute attack`,`Avoid in sulpha allergy, sickle cell, renal failure`],[`Timolol 0.5% drops`,`1 drop stat, then twice daily`,`Acute attack`,`Not in asthma, COPD, heart block`]]},{heading:`Ophthalmia neonatorum by day of onset`,columns:[`Day of onset`,`Cause`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Day 1`,`Chemical`,`Supportive care`],[`Days 2-5`,`Gonococcal`,`Ceftriaxone, saline lavage, admit`],[`Days 5-14`,`Chlamydial`,`Oral erythromycin 14 days; treat parents`]]}],remember:[`**Acuity before anything** - normal acuity almost excludes sight-threatening causes.`,`Itch means allergy; photophobia means cornea or iris.`,`Never prescribe a steroid drop without a slit lamp.`,`Never send a patient home with a topical anaesthetic.`,`Vomiting with one-sided headache in the elderly - look at the pupil.`,`One drop is enough - the sac holds only 7-10 microlitres.`,`Treat the fellow eye in angle closure with laser iridotomy.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - The red eye`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Visible dilatation of **conjunctival, episcleral or ciliary vessels**; the layer that is dilated names the diagnosis. Split: **red eye with normal vision and no true pain** (OPD) versus **red eye with reduced vision, pain or photophobia** (refer).`],[`Relevant classification`,`By injection: **conjunctival** (fornices, blanches), **ciliary/circumcorneal** (limbus - cornea, iris, ciliary body), mixed. Conjunctivitis by cause (bacterial, viral, chlamydial, allergic, toxic); uveitis by **SUN** anatomy; ophthalmia neonatorum = first 28 days; trachoma WHO simplified grading (India **eliminated 2024**).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`About **80% of GP red eyes are conjunctivitis**; contact lens wear (Pseudomonas, Acanthamoeba), **vegetative trauma** (fungal), OTC **steroid-antibiotic drops**, hypermetropic elderly eye + dim light/anticholinergic/topiramate (angle closure), HLA-B27, RA/GPA (scleritis), recent surgery (endophthalmitis).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Surface inflammation -> conjunctival vessels, grittiness, discharge; corneal epithelial loss -> **trigeminal pain, fluorescein uptake**; iris/ciliary inflammation -> **trigemino-ciliary photophobia**, flare, KPs, synechiae; pupil block -> iris bombe -> **IOP 40-80 mmHg** -> corneal oedema, fixed mid-dilated pupil, vomiting.`],[`Key history`,`**Five questions**: blurred vision, true pain vs grittiness, discharge type, photophobia, one or both eyes. Itch = allergy; purulent stuck lids = bacterial; watery + URTI + node = adenovirus. Ask contact lens, vegetative trauma, drops used, haloes/vomiting, back pain, recent surgery, neonate day of onset.`],[`Key examination`,`**Visual acuity first** with pinhole; torch exam; **oblique shadow test** before dilating; evert upper lid; **fluorescein + cobalt blue** (dendrite = HSV); pupil shape/RAPD; **digital tension**; preauricular node; **phenylephrine 2.5% blanching** (episcleritis vs scleritis); red reflex; BP, joints, skin.`],[`Investigations`,`**Simple conjunctivitis - no test**. Swab Gram stain/culture/chlamydia NAAT in neonates (**Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** = treat same day); corneal scrape + **KOH** before drops; tonometry (**over 40 mmHg** in attack); recurrent uveitis: HLA-B27, CXR, IGRA, VDRL; scleritis: RF, anti-CCP, ANCA.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Bacterial: **chloramphenicol 0.5% 2-hourly x 48 h then QID + 1% ointment at night, 5 days**; lens wearer: **moxifloxacin 0.5% TDS 7 days**. Adenovirus: preservative-free lubricant, cold compress, no antibiotic. Allergic: **olopatadine 0.1% BD** or ketotifen; cromoglicate 2% QID started before season.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Gonococcal: **ceftriaxone 1 g IM** (neonate 25-50 mg/kg, max 125 mg). Chlamydia: **azithromycin 1 g** adult; neonate erythromycin 50 mg/kg/day x 14 d. HSV: **acyclovir 3% ointment 5x/day x 14 d** or ganciclovir 0.15% gel. Uveitis (ophthalmologist): **prednisolone acetate 1% 1-2 hourly**, taper 4-6 weeks + cyclopentolate/homatropine.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**One drop** (drop 30-50 microlitre, sac holds 7-10); pull lower lid, close eye 1-2 min, **punctal occlusion**; **5 min between drops**, ointment last (1 cm ribbon); discard bottle **4 weeks after opening**; never touch tip to eye.`],[`Special groups`,`Neonate: day 1 chemical, **2-5 gonococcal, 5-14 chlamydial** - systemic treatment, treat both parents. Children: fusidic acid BD. Pregnancy: chloramphenicol, fusidic acid, erythromycin acceptable. **Brimonidine contraindicated under 2 years**; punctal occlusion in infants.`],[`Complications`,`Corneal ulcer/perforation (gonococcus in 24-48 h), **steroid glaucoma** (1 in 3 responders) and cataract, geographic HSV ulcer with steroid, synechiae, secondary glaucoma, fellow-eye angle closure (**40-80%**), endophthalmitis, scleral melt.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Same day**: reduced acuity, true pain, photophobia, corneal opacity/staining, abnormal pupil, hard globe, hypopyon/hyphaema, painful lens-wearer eye, red eye within 6 weeks of surgery. **Within hours**: angle closure, chemical injury after irrigation, endophthalmitis, penetrating injury, neonatal gonococcus.`],[`Emergency - angle closure`,`Supine, analgesia, antiemetic, **acetazolamide 500 mg IV/oral**, timolol 0.5%, brimonidine 0.2%, prednisolone acetate 1%, **pilocarpine 2% once IOP falls**, mannitol 1-2 g/kg IV if needed -> **laser peripheral iridotomy both eyes**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Return if bacterial not better in **5-7 days**, adenoviral **2-3 weeks**; adenovirus contagious **10-14 days**; anyone on topical steroid over 2 weeks needs **IOP and lens check**; record acuity every visit.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Hand hygiene, separate towels, no shared drops; stop lens wear, never tap water; no OTC steroid drops or home remedies; SAFE strategy and trichiasis surveillance (trachoma); ocular prophylaxis and maternal STI care for neonates.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No major change to 2026: **AAO PPP Conjunctivitis 2023** and NICE CKS 2024 remain current (delayed antibiotic acceptable, chloramphenicol first line); **WHO validated India trachoma-free in 2024** - trichiasis surveillance continues.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Acuity before anything**; itch = allergy; photophobia = cornea or iris; **never steroid drops without a slit lamp**; never prescribe topical anaesthetic; vomiting + unilateral headache in elderly - look at the pupil; one drop is enough.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Red eye in the OPD - triage in five steps`,caption:`Condensed bedside algorithm; any 'yes' at a decision point means refer.`,steps:[{label:`Measure acuity each eye, with pinhole`,detail:`Unexplained drop in acuity = sight-threatening until proved otherwise`,tone:`decision`},{label:`True pain, photophobia or ciliary flush?`,detail:`Points to keratitis, uveitis, scleritis or angle closure`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Fluorescein, pupil, digital tension`,detail:`Stain/infiltrate, irregular or fixed mid-dilated pupil, stony hard eye`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Normal vision, gritty, conjunctival injection`,detail:`Itch = allergy; pus = bacterial; watery + node = adenovirus`},{label:`Treat in OPD, no steroid`,detail:`Chloramphenicol 0.5% (5 days) or lubricants or olopatadine 0.1% BD`},{label:`Safety-net and review`,detail:`Return same day for pain, light sensitivity, blur; review 5-7 days`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Five causes of red eye at a glance`,columns:[`Feature`,`Conjunctivitis`,`Keratitis`,`Anterior uveitis`,`Angle closure`],rows:[[`Vision`,`Normal`,`Reduced`,`Mildly reduced`,`Markedly reduced, haloes`],[`Injection`,`Conjunctival`,`Ciliary`,`Ciliary`,`Mixed`],[`Cornea`,`Clear`,`Infiltrate, stains`,`KPs`,`Hazy, oedematous`],[`Pupil`,`Normal`,`Normal or small`,`Small, irregular`,`Mid-dilated, oval, fixed`],[`Tension`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal or low`,`Stony hard`],[`Action`,`Treat in OPD`,`Refer same day, no drops`,`Refer same day`,`Treat and refer within hours`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Acute angle closure - first aid while transferring`,steps:[{label:`Lie supine, analgesia, antiemetic`,detail:`Vomiting is trigemino-vagal, not gastritis`},{label:`Acetazolamide 500 mg IV or oral`,detail:`Then 250 mg 6-hourly; avoid in sulpha allergy, sickle cell, renal failure`},{label:`Timolol 0.5% + brimonidine 0.2% + prednisolone 1%`,detail:`One drop each; timolol not in asthma, COPD, heart block`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Pilocarpine 2% once IOP starts to fall`,detail:`Ischaemic sphincter will not respond above 40-50 mmHg`},{label:`Mannitol 20% 1-2 g/kg IV if IOP stays high`,detail:`Over 30-45 min; caution heart and renal failure`},{label:`Laser peripheral iridotomy both eyes`,detail:`Fellow eye has 40-80% risk without it`,tone:`good`}]}]},"eye-ent-otitis":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**AOM** is rapid-onset middle ear inflammation with an effusion.`,`AOM is diagnosed by a **moderate or severe bulging drum** or new otorrhoea.`,`**OME (glue ear)** is fluid behind an intact drum with no acute inflammation.`,`**Otitis externa** is canal inflammation under 6 weeks with pain on tragal pressure.`,`**CSOM** is a perforated drum discharging for **more than 2 weeks** (WHO).`,`**Recurrent AOM** is 3 episodes in 6 months or 4 in 12 months.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Mucosal CSOM** (tubotympanic, safe) has a central perforation with a rim all round.`,`**Squamous CSOM** (atticoantral, unsafe) has cholesteatoma with an attic or marginal perforation.`,`Each type is active (discharging) or inactive.`,`Only the squamous type erodes bone and causes intracranial complications.`,`**Malignant otitis externa** is Pseudomonas skull-base osteomyelitis in elderly diabetics.`,`AOM organisms: **S. pneumoniae, non-typeable H. influenzae and Moraxella**.`,`AOM risks: age 6-24 months, **supine bottle feeding**, day care, smoke, cleft palate.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A cold or big adenoids **block the Eustachian tube**, which is short and horizontal in infants.`,`2. Middle ear air is absorbed, creating negative pressure and a retracted drum.`,`3. Fluid collects and nasopharyngeal bacteria multiply in it.`,`4. Pus builds up, giving a **red bulging drum**, severe pain and fever.`,`5. The drum may perforate; sudden pain relief with discharge means perforation, not cure.`,`6. About **80% settle within 3 days** without antibiotics.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Throbbing earache after a cold with fever suggests **AOM**.`,`Pain worse on chewing after swimming or cotton buds suggests **otitis externa**.`,`Infants show ear tugging, irritability, night waking and poor feeding.`,`Profuse odourless mucoid discharge suggests **safe CSOM**.`,`Scanty **foul-smelling discharge** suggests cholesteatoma.`,`Always ask about vertigo, facial weakness, headache and neck stiffness.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Pull the pinna **up and back in adults**, down and back in infants.`,`**Tragal tenderness** is the key sign of otitis externa.`,`Look at the **attic last and always**; that is where cholesteatoma hides.`,`**Pneumatic otoscopy**: an immobile drum confirms effusion; best bedside test.`,`A red drum in a crying child proves nothing.`,`Check the facial nerve; any weakness with ear disease is an emergency.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`AOM and uncomplicated otitis externa are **clinical diagnoses**; no tests needed.`,`**Tympanometry type B** (flat) with normal volume means effusion.`,`Pure tone audiometry for OME lasting 3 months and before CSOM surgery.`,`Blood sugar or HbA1c in recurrent otitis externa and every malignant otitis externa.`,`**HRCT temporal bone** for unsafe CSOM and any complication.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Analgesia first**: paracetamol 15 mg/kg or ibuprofen 10 mg/kg, by the clock.`,`Immediate antibiotic if **under 6 months**, bilateral under 2 years, otorrhoea or severe.`,`Severe means moderate-severe pain, pain over 48 hours or temperature 39 C or more.`,`Others: observe 48-72 hours with a **delayed (back-up) prescription**.`,`**Amoxicillin 80-90 mg/kg/day** in 2 doses; high dose beats pneumococcal resistance.`,`Otitis externa: **aural toilet plus ciprofloxacin-dexamethasone drops**; no oral antibiotic.`,`OME: watchful waiting for **3 months**; no decongestants, antihistamines or antibiotics.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Use **co-amoxiclav** if amoxicillin in the last 30 days or failure at 48-72 hours.`,`Failure of two antibiotics: **ceftriaxone 50 mg/kg** once daily for 1-3 days.`,`Active mucosal CSOM: daily dry mopping plus **quinolone ear drops** for 10-14 days.`,`Otomycosis: suction clearance, then **clotrimazole 1% drops**; stop antibacterial drops.`,`Malignant otitis externa: admit, control sugar, antipseudomonal antibiotics for **6-8 weeks**.`,`**Grommets** for OME over 3 months with hearing worse than 25-30 dB or speech delay.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Acute mastoiditis**: postaural swelling with the pinna pushed down, out and forward.`,`Fever, headache, neck stiffness or drowsiness means an **intracranial complication**; admit.`,`Vertigo or positive fistula test means a **labyrinthine fistula**; do not syringe.`,`Never put **aminoglycoside drops through a perforation**; they are ototoxic.`,`Unilateral effusion in an adult needs exclusion of **nasopharyngeal carcinoma**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months and **feeding the infant upright**.`,`Avoid household tobacco and biomass smoke.`,`**PCV** and annual influenza vaccine reduce AOM episodes.`,`Dry-ear care: no swimming, vaseline cotton while bathing, no oil or cotton buds.`,`School screening under the **NPPCD** detects hidden conductive loss.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of otitis at the bedside`,columns:[`Feature`,`AOM`,`Otitis externa`,`Safe CSOM`,`Unsafe CSOM`],rows:[[`Pain`,`Severe, eases on perforation`,`Severe, on chewing`,`Usually painless`,`Painless; pain means complication`],[`Tragal tenderness`,`Absent`,`Present`,`Absent`,`Absent`],[`Discharge`,`Only after perforation`,`Scanty, no mucus`,`Profuse, mucoid, odourless`,`Scanty, foul, may bleed`],[`Drum`,`Red, bulging, immobile`,`Intact, hidden by oedema`,`Central perforation`,`Attic or marginal perforation, keratin`],[`Treatment`,`Analgesia; amoxicillin by rule`,`Toilet, steroid-quinolone drops`,`Toilet, quinolone drops, tympanoplasty`,`HRCT, mastoidectomy`]]},{heading:`Drugs in otitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`15 mg/kg 6-hourly oral (max 60 mg/kg/day)`,`48-72 hours by the clock`,`Liver toxicity in overdose`],[`Ibuprofen`,`10 mg/kg 8-hourly after food`,`48-72 hours`,`Avoid in dehydration, peptic ulcer`],[`Amoxicillin`,`80-90 mg/kg/day oral in 2 doses`,`10 d under 2 y; 7 d at 2-5 y; 5-7 d from 6 y`,`Diarrhoea, rash`],[`Co-amoxiclav`,`90/6.4 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`10 days`,`Diarrhoea; take at start of meal`],[`Azithromycin`,`10 mg/kg once daily`,`3 days`,`Penicillin anaphylaxis only; QT prolongation`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`50 mg/kg IM or IV once daily`,`1-3 days`,`After two antibiotic failures`],[`Ciprofloxacin 0.3% + dexamethasone ear drops`,`4 drops twice daily`,`7-10 days`,`Safe through perforation`],[`Ofloxacin 0.3% ear drops`,`10 drops twice daily (CSOM)`,`14 days`,`Non-ototoxic; after aural toilet`],[`Clotrimazole 1% ear drops`,`3-4 drops three times daily`,`14 days after symptoms settle`,`Local burning`]]},{heading:`Tympanometry patterns`,columns:[`Type`,`What it shows`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Type A`,`Normal peak`,`Normal middle ear`],[`Type B, normal volume`,`Flat trace`,`Effusion (OME or AOM)`],[`Type B, large volume`,`Flat trace`,`Perforation or patent grommet`],[`Type C`,`Negative pressure peak`,`Eustachian tube dysfunction`],[`Type As / Ad`,`Shallow / very high peak`,`Otosclerosis / ossicular discontinuity`]]}],remember:[`Redness proves nothing - a **bulging, immobile drum** proves AOM.`,`Tragal pain is otitis externa; pain relieved by discharge is perforated AOM.`,`First prescription in AOM is **regular analgesia**.`,`The **dose of amoxicillin**, not a newer drug, beats resistance.`,`Central perforation with mucoid discharge is safe; attic with foul discharge is unsafe.`,`Never aminoglycoside drops through a perforation.`,`Warm ear drops in the palm - cold drops cause giddiness.`,`Earache with a normal ear is referred - check teeth, TMJ, tonsil, larynx.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - AOM, otitis externa, OME and CSOM`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**AOM**: rapid-onset middle ear inflammation with effusion - **moderate/severe bulging drum**, new otorrhoea, or mild bulging + pain under 48 h. **OME**: fluid behind intact drum, no inflammation. **OE**: canal inflammation under 6 weeks with **tragal pain**. **CSOM**: perforation discharging **over 2 weeks** (WHO).`],[`Relevant classification`,`CSOM **mucosal (tubotympanic, safe, central perforation)** vs **squamous (atticoantral, unsafe, cholesteatoma, attic/marginal)**, active or inactive. **Recurrent AOM**: 3 in 6 months or 4 in 12 months. Malignant OE = skull-base osteomyelitis.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`AOM: **S. pneumoniae, NTHi, Moraxella**; age 6-24 months, **supine bottle feeding**, no breastfeeding, day care, smoke/biomass, cleft palate, Down syndrome, adenoids. OE: **Pseudomonas, S. aureus**; water, cotton buds, eczema. Malignant OE: **elderly diabetic**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`URTI -> **Eustachian tube block** (infant tube short, wide, horizontal) -> negative pressure -> effusion -> bacterial suppuration -> bulging -> perforation (pain eases, discharge). Cholesteatoma: retraction pocket -> keratin -> **RANKL/collagenase bone erosion** -> ossicles, facial canal, lateral canal, tegmen.`],[`Key history`,`Otalgia (chewing/tragal pain = OE), ear tugging in infants, **discharge character** (profuse mucoid = safe; **scanty foul** = unsafe), duration, hearing/speech, **complication questions** (vertigo, facial weakness, headache, neck stiffness, postaural swelling), antibiotic in last 30 days, diabetes.`],[`Key examination`,`Postaural region, **tragal pressure**, mastoid tenderness; otoscopy (pinna up-back adult, **down-back infant**), drum colour/position, **attic last**; **pneumatic otoscopy** (immobile = effusion); 512 Hz Rinne/Weber; fistula test; facial nerve; nose and postnasal space.`],[`Investigations`,`AOM/uncomplicated OE - **clinical**. Tympanometry: **Type B** effusion, C tube dysfunction. PTA if OME 3 months or pre-surgery. Swab only in non-responding CSOM. **HbA1c** in recurrent OE. **HRCT temporal bone** for unsafe CSOM; malignant OE: ESR/CRP, CT + MRI.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Analgesia first**: paracetamol 15 mg/kg 6-hourly or ibuprofen 10 mg/kg 8-hourly. **Antibiotic immediately if under 6 months, 6-23 months bilateral, otorrhoea, severe (39 C or more, pain over 48 h)**; else observe 48-72 h / delayed script. **Amoxicillin 80-90 mg/kg/day in 2 doses**: 10 days under 2 y, 7 days 2-5 y, 5-7 days 6 y+.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Co-amoxiclav 90/6.4 mg/kg/day** if amoxicillin in last 30 days, otitis-conjunctivitis or failure at 48-72 h; non-anaphylactic allergy **cefuroxime 30 mg/kg/day**; anaphylaxis azithromycin 10 mg/kg x 3 d; two failures **ceftriaxone 50 mg/kg IM x 1-3 days**; myringotomy.`],[`Treatment - otitis externa & CSOM`,`OE: aural toilet + **ciprofloxacin 0.3%-dexamethasone 0.1% 4 drops BD x 7 days** or ofloxacin 10 drops OD; wick if canal shut; **no oral antibiotic**. Otomycosis: clotrimazole 1%. Mucosal CSOM: dry mopping + **quinolone drops BD 10-14 days**. **No aminoglycoside drops through a perforation**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Ear drops: **warm bottle in palm**, ear up, pull pinna, drops along wall, **tragal pumping**, lie 3-5 min; clean canal first; ear wick changed 2-3 days; **dry mopping** with cotton wisp; autoinflation balloon for OME from 3-4 years.`],[`Special groups`,`Infant under 3 months with fever = febrile young infant work-up. **Down syndrome and cleft palate** - early referral (aids often preferred). Pregnancy: amoxicillin, co-amoxiclav, cefuroxime, topical quinolone; no systemic quinolone. Diabetic elderly: malignant OE.`],[`Complications`,`Mastoiditis, **meningitis (commonest intracranial)**, brain abscess, lateral sinus thrombosis (picket-fence fever), facial palsy, labyrinthine fistula, Gradenigo, Bezold abscess, conductive hearing loss and speech delay.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Postaural swelling with pinna pushed forward; fever + headache + neck stiffness; **facial weakness**; vertigo/positive fistula test; foul scanty discharge with attic perforation; **night otalgia + canal-floor granulation in diabetic**; unilateral OME in adult (**exclude nasopharyngeal carcinoma**).`],[`Treatment - malignant OE`,`Admit, glycaemic control, **IV ceftazidime 2 g 8-hourly or piperacillin-tazobactam**, or oral ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD early, **6-8 weeks**, monitor ESR/CRP; facial palsy first, then IX-XI.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`AOM review 48-72 h if not improving and **6-12 weeks** for effusion/perforation; **OME: test hearing at 3 months**, grommets if bilateral loss worse than **25-30 dB** or speech/learning issues; OE review 7 days; perforation over 6 weeks refer.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Exclusive breastfeeding**, upright feeding, smoke-free home, **PCV and influenza vaccine**, dry-ear care, no cotton buds/oil; **NPPCD** school screening; treat the nose (AR, adenoids).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No new guideline to 2026: **AAP 2013 AOM rule** (still current), **NICE NG91 2018** (back-up prescribing), **NICE NG233 2023** (OME - watch 3 months, grommets for 25-30 dB loss) and **AAO-HNS OE 2014** (drops, no oral antibiotic) remain the exam answers.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Red drum in a crying child proves nothing - **bulging/immobile drum** proves AOM; tragal pain = OE; **high-dose amoxicillin beats resistance**; OE needs drops not tablets; central perforation safe, attic/marginal unsafe; look at the attic last and always.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute otitis media - antibiotic or wait`,caption:`AAP 2013 rule, still current; analgesia for everyone.`,steps:[{label:`Confirm AOM: bulging or immobile drum`,detail:`Pneumatic otoscopy; red drum alone is not AOM`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Regular analgesia by the clock 48-72 h`,detail:`Paracetamol 15 mg/kg 6-hourly or ibuprofen 10 mg/kg 8-hourly`},{label:`Immediate antibiotic criteria?`,detail:`Under 6 months; 6-23 months bilateral; otorrhoea; 39 C or more; pain over 48 h`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Amoxicillin 80-90 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,detail:`10 days under 2 y; 7 days 2-5 y; 5-7 days 6 y and over`},{label:`No better at 48-72 h`,detail:`Co-amoxiclav 90/6.4 mg/kg/day; then ceftriaxone 50 mg/kg IM 1-3 days`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Review drum at 6-12 weeks`,detail:`Persistent effusion = OME pathway; hearing test at 3 months`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`The painful or discharging ear`,columns:[`Feature`,`AOM`,`Otitis externa`,`CSOM safe`,`CSOM unsafe`],rows:[[`Pain`,`Severe, eases on perforation`,`Severe, tragal`,`Painless`,`Painless; pain = complication`],[`Discharge`,`Mucopurulent after perforation`,`Scanty, no mucus`,`Profuse mucoid odourless`,`Scanty foul, blood-stained`],[`Drum`,`Red, bulging, immobile`,`Intact, hidden by oedema`,`Central perforation`,`Attic/marginal, keratin, polyp`],[`Treatment`,`Analgesia; amoxicillin by rule`,`Aural toilet, steroid-quinolone drops`,`Dry ear, quinolone drops, tympanoplasty`,`HRCT, mastoidectomy`]]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Complications of otitis media - where the pus goes`,root:`Discharging ear with a new symptom`,arms:[{label:`Intratemporal`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Mastoiditis - pinna pushed forward`,`Facial palsy`,`Labyrinthine fistula - vertigo`,`Petrous apicitis (Gradenigo)`]},{label:`Intracranial`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Meningitis (commonest)`,`Temporal lobe or cerebellar abscess`,`Lateral sinus thrombosis - picket-fence fever`,`Extradural abscess`]},{label:`Extracranial`,steps:[`Subperiosteal abscess`,`Bezold abscess in sternomastoid`,`Admit, IV ceftriaxone + metronidazole, CT/MRI`]}]}]},"eye-ent-sore-throat":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute pharyngitis** is inflammation of the pharynx and tonsils, usually lasting under a week.`,`Most sore throats are **viral**: 70-85% in adults and 60-70% in children.`,`**GABHS** causes 15-30% in children aged 5-15 and 5-15% in adults.`,`In India we treat GABHS to **prevent rheumatic fever**, not for symptom relief.`,`Antibiotics shorten the sore throat by only about **16 hours**.`,`GABHS is uncommon under 3 years, so routine testing there is not needed.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`The key split is **viral versus GABHS**.`,`**McIsaac score** decides who is tested (IDSA 2025).`,`McIsaac **0-1**: no test, no antibiotic.`,`McIsaac **2-3**: rapid antigen test or culture; treat only if positive.`,`McIsaac **4-5**: test, or treat empirically in high rheumatic fever settings like India.`,`Recurrent tonsillitis for surgery uses the **Paradise criteria (7-5-3)**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Viruses inflame the whole airway, so they bring **cough, coryza and hoarseness**.`,`2. GABHS sticks to pharyngeal cells; its **M protein** resists phagocytosis.`,`3. Pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens and cause the **scarlet fever** rash.`,`4. Two to four weeks later, **molecular mimicry** causes rheumatic fever.`,`5. Treatment **within 9 days** of onset still prevents rheumatic fever.`,`6. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is **not prevented** by antibiotics.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Inability to swallow saliva** or drooling means airway or deep neck space.`,`Cough, coryza, hoarseness and conjunctivitis point to a **virus**.`,`Sudden onset, headache, abdominal pain and sandpaper rash favour GABHS.`,`Ask **immunisation status**; diphtheria still occurs in India.`,`Ask about **carbimazole, clozapine or methotrexate**: sore throat may be agranulocytosis.`,`Adult smoker with persistent **one-sided** sore throat: cancer until proved otherwise.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Assess the airway first**; with stridor or drooling, do not use a spatula.`,`Look for exudate, tonsil size and **tender anterior cervical nodes**.`,`Posterior nodes with splenomegaly suggest **EBV**.`,`Trismus, palatal bulge and **uvula pushed to the opposite side** mean quinsy.`,`Grey membrane that **bleeds on removal** with bull neck means diphtheria.`,`Neck stiffness with a posterior pharyngeal bulge in a young child: retropharyngeal abscess.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No test for McIsaac 0-1** or a clear viral picture.`,`Rapid antigen test is specific; a **negative RADT in a child needs a culture**.`,`**Throat culture** on blood agar is the gold standard.`,`**ASO titre has no role** in acute pharyngitis; it rises 2-3 weeks later.`,`Urgent CBC for neutrophils in anyone on a drug that can cause agranulocytosis.`,`Never wait for diphtheria swab results before giving **antitoxin**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Regular analgesia by the clock**: paracetamol or ibuprofen.`,`Warm saline gargles, fluids and soft cool foods.`,`**Penicillin V** is first line for GABHS, for **10 days**.`,`**Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg once daily** (max 1 g) for 10 days is the practical alternative.`,`Finish all 10 days even when better by day 3.`,`**Benzathine penicillin** single IM dose if adherence is doubtful.`,`Never prescribe cephalosporins, quinolones or co-amoxiclav for simple pharyngitis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Non-anaphylactic penicillin allergy: **cephalexin** for 10 days.`,`Anaphylactic allergy: **azithromycin for 5 days** or clindamycin for 10 days.`,`**Never give amoxicillin** when infectious mononucleosis is possible; 80-90% get a rash.`,`Quinsy: **aspiration or drainage** plus IV antibiotics the same day.`,`Diphtheria: **antitoxin** plus erythromycin or penicillin for 14 days; isolate and notify.`,`After rheumatic fever: **benzathine penicillin 1.2 MU every 3 weeks** in India.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Epiglottitis**: drooling, stridor, tripod posture; no spatula, secure the airway.`,`**Lemierre syndrome**: sore throat, rigors, neck tenderness, pleuritic pain.`,`Sore throat with fever on carbimazole or clozapine: treat as **febrile neutropenia** if neutrophils low.`,`Watch for **rheumatic fever at 2-4 weeks** and glomerulonephritis at 1-2 weeks.`,`No contact sport for **4-6 weeks** after infectious mononucleosis (splenic rupture).`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **48-72 hours** if not improving; penicillin failure means wrong diagnosis or non-adherence.`,`**Document each episode**; Paradise criteria need documented episodes.`,`Tonsillectomy for Paradise criteria, **two quinsy episodes** or obstructive sleep apnoea.`,`Refer adults with persistent **unilateral tonsil enlargement** for biopsy.`,`Return to school **24 hours after** starting an effective antibiotic.`]}],tables:[{heading:`McIsaac score`,columns:[`Component`,`Finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`Tonsils`,`Exudate or swelling`,`1`],[`Anterior cervical nodes`,`Tender or enlarged`,`1`],[`Fever`,`Over 38 C`,`1`],[`Cough`,`Absent`,`1`],[`Age`,`3-14 years`,`1`],[`Age`,`15-44 years`,`0`],[`Age`,`45 years or older`,`-1`]]},{heading:`Drugs in sore throat`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`Adult 0.5-1 g 6-hourly; child 15 mg/kg`,`2-3 days by the clock`,`Liver damage in overdose`],[`Ibuprofen`,`Adult 400 mg 8-hourly; child 10 mg/kg`,`2-3 days, after food`,`Avoid if dehydrated, asthma, CKD`],[`Penicillin V`,`Adult 500 mg BD; under 27 kg 250 mg BD-TDS`,`10 days`,`1 hour before food; rash`],[`Amoxicillin`,`50 mg/kg once daily, max 1 g`,`10 days`,`Florid rash in EBV`],[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`1.2 MU IM (600,000 U under 27 kg)`,`Single dose; RF prophylaxis 3-weekly`,`Anaphylaxis rare; adrenaline ready`],[`Cephalexin`,`20 mg/kg BD, max 500 mg a dose`,`10 days`,`Non-anaphylactic allergy only`],[`Azithromycin`,`12 mg/kg once daily, max 500 mg`,`5 days`,`QT prolongation; rising resistance`],[`Diphtheria antitoxin`,`20,000-100,000 units IV by severity`,`Single dose`,`Sensitivity test first`]]},{heading:`Dangerous sore throats`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Immediate action`],rows:[[`Quinsy`,`Trismus, hot potato voice, uvular deviation`,`Drainage plus IV antibiotics today`],[`Epiglottitis`,`Drooling, stridor, tripod posture`,`No spatula; secure airway`],[`Retropharyngeal abscess`,`Young child, neck stiffness, torticollis`,`Admit, CT, drainage`],[`Diphtheria`,`Bleeding grey membrane, bull neck`,`Antitoxin, isolate, notify`],[`Infectious mononucleosis`,`Membrane, posterior nodes, spleen`,`No amoxicillin; CBC, monospot`],[`Agranulocytosis`,`Fever on carbimazole or clozapine`,`Stop drug; urgent CBC`]]}],remember:[`Cough, coryza and hoarseness argue for a **virus**.`,`S. pyogenes has **never been resistant to penicillin**.`,`Treat GABHS within 9 days and you still prevent rheumatic fever.`,`Antibiotics prevent rheumatic fever, **not glomerulonephritis**.`,`**No amoxicillin** if glandular fever is possible.`,`Trismus with sore throat is **quinsy** until proved otherwise.`,`Tonsillectomy by **Paradise 7-5-3**, with documented episodes.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`IDSA 2025 guideline update (Part 1): a clinical score (Centor or McIsaac) is now recommended in children and adults to decide who needs a GAS test; treatment advice of IDSA 2012 unchanged pending Part 2`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Sore throat and streptococcal tonsillitis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute pharyngitis/tonsillopharyngitis: pharyngeal and tonsillar inflammation with sore throat and odynophagia, usually under 1 week; **70-85% adult and 60-70% child cases viral**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Viral vs GABHS** (15-30% at 5-15 years, 5-15% adults, rare under 3). **McIsaac**: exudate/swelling, tender anterior nodes, fever over 38 C, no cough, **age 3-14 +1, 45+ -1**. FeverPAIN (NICE). Recurrent tonsillitis: **Paradise 7-5-3**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Viruses (rhino, adeno, corona, influenza, **EBV**); S. pyogenes (M protein, streptolysins, superantigens); Fusobacterium (Lemierre); C. diphtheriae in unimmunised; drug agranulocytosis (**carbimazole, clozapine, methotrexate**).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`GABHS adhesion -> M protein resists phagocytosis -> exudative tonsillitis -> **molecular mimicry (M protein vs cardiac myosin, valves, basal ganglia)** -> rheumatic fever 2-4 weeks later; **treatment within 9 days prevents ARF**; PSGN (immune complex) **not prevented**.`],[`Key history`,`Cough, coryza, hoarseness, conjunctivitis = viral; sudden onset, headache, abdominal pain, rash = GABHS; **drooling, trismus, hot potato voice, stridor** = deep space/airway; immunisation (diphtheria); past RF; drugs causing agranulocytosis; adult smoker with unilateral symptoms = cancer.`],[`Key examination`,`**Airway first - no spatula if drooling or stridor**; hydration, SpO2; tonsils (exudate, membrane), **tender anterior nodes** (GABHS) vs posterior nodes + spleen (EBV); **uvula deviated** (quinsy); grey bleeding membrane, bull neck (diphtheria); scarlatiniform rash, strawberry tongue; heart murmur.`],[`Investigations`,`**Score first, then test (IDSA 2025)**: McIsaac 0-1 no test. **RADT** specificity 90-95%, sensitivity 70-90% - **negative in a child needs culture**; culture gold standard. **No ASO** for acute pharyngitis; no test of cure. CBC/monospot for EBV; urgent neutrophils on agranulocytosis drugs; Albert stain for diphtheria.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**0-1**: no test, no antibiotic. **2-3**: RADT/culture, treat if positive (delayed script if no test). **4-5 (about 50%)**: test, or **treat empirically in India** (high RF setting).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Analgesia by the clock (paracetamol 15 mg/kg, ibuprofen 10 mg/kg). **Penicillin V: under 27 kg 250 mg BD-TDS; adult 500 mg BD x 10 days**, 1 h before food; or **amoxicillin 50 mg/kg once daily (max 1 g) x 10 days**; **benzathine penicillin 0.6 MU (under 27 kg) / 1.2 MU deep IM** if adherence doubtful.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Non-anaphylactic allergy: **cephalexin 20 mg/kg BD x 10 days**; anaphylaxis: **azithromycin 12 mg/kg OD x 5 days** or clindamycin 7 mg/kg TDS x 10 days. No 3rd-gen cephalosporin, quinolone or co-amoxiclav for simple pharyngitis. Failure = adherence or wrong diagnosis.`],[`Treatment - deep space & special infections`,`**Quinsy**: aspirate/drain + IV co-amoxiclav 1.2 g 8-hourly or penicillin + metronidazole. **Epiglottitis**: secure airway, ceftriaxone. **Diphtheria**: antitoxin 20,000-100,000 U by extent + erythromycin, isolate, notify. **Lemierre**: anaerobic cover 4-6 weeks.`],[`Special groups`,`Under 3 years - no routine testing. **EBV (teenager)** - never amoxicillin (80-90% rash), no contact sport 4-6 weeks. Pregnancy: penicillin V, amoxicillin, cephalexin, azithromycin. Past RF: treat any GAS throat; **secondary prophylaxis BPG 1.2 MU every 3 weeks**.`],[`Complications`,`Suppurative: quinsy, parapharyngeal/retropharyngeal abscess, Lemierre. Non-suppurative: **acute rheumatic fever (2-4 weeks)**, **PSGN (1-2 weeks)**, scarlet fever, streptococcal toxic shock.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Stridor, drooling, tripod, inability to swallow saliva; trismus + uvular deviation; neck stiffness/torticollis in toddler; grey membrane; rigors + neck tenderness; fever on carbimazole/clozapine; **persistent unilateral tonsil ulcer in adult - urgent biopsy (NICE NG12)**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review 48-72 h if not improving; **document every episode** (Paradise needs documented episodes); watch for ARF and PSGN; RF register, trace defaulters; prophylaxis 5 years or to 21 (no carditis), 10 years or to 21 (mild), to 40 or lifelong (severe).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Tonsillectomy - Paradise: 7 in 1 year, 5/year for 2 years, 3/year for 3 years** (documented); 2 episodes of quinsy; obstructive sleep-disordered breathing with large tonsils; suspected malignancy.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IDSA 2025 (Part 1)**: use Centor/McIsaac in children and adults before testing - reduces needless swabs and antibiotics; penicillin/amoxicillin 10 days unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Cough and coryza argue against strep; **S. pyogenes never penicillin-resistant**; antibiotics prevent ARF not PSGN; treatment within 9 days still prevents ARF; ASO has no role in acute pharyngitis; quinsy = trismus + uvula pushed away.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Sore throat in primary care (IDSA 2025 score-first)`,steps:[{label:`Airway check: drooling, stridor, trismus?`,detail:`Yes = no spatula; deep space or epiglottitis; admit`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Calculate McIsaac score`,detail:`Exudate, tender anterior nodes, fever, no cough; age 3-14 +1, 45+ -1`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Score 0-1: no test, no antibiotic`,detail:`Analgesia, fluids, gargles, safety-net`},{label:`Score 2-3: RADT or culture`,detail:`Treat only if positive; negative RADT in child - culture`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Score 4-5: test or treat empirically in India`,detail:`Rheumatic fever prevention is the goal`},{label:`Penicillin V or amoxicillin for 10 days`,detail:`Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg OD max 1 g; BPG once if adherence doubtful`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Antibiotic choice for GAS pharyngitis`,steps:[{label:`Penicillin V 10 days`,detail:`Under 27 kg 250 mg BD-TDS; adult 500 mg BD; 1 h before food`,tone:`good`},{label:`Amoxicillin 10 days`,detail:`50 mg/kg once daily (max 1 g) or 25 mg/kg BD`},{label:`Benzathine penicillin G deep IM once`,detail:`600,000 U under 27 kg; 1.2 MU at 27 kg or more; observe 30 min`},{label:`Non-anaphylactic allergy: cephalexin`,detail:`20 mg/kg BD (max 500 mg) x 10 days`},{label:`Anaphylaxis: azithromycin or clindamycin`,detail:`Azithro 12 mg/kg OD x 5 d; clinda 7 mg/kg TDS x 10 d`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Dangerous sore throats - clue and action`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Action`],rows:[[`Quinsy`,`Trismus, hot potato voice, uvula pushed away`,`Aspirate or drain + IV antibiotics today`],[`Epiglottitis`,`Drooling, stridor, tripod, normal oropharynx`,`No spatula; secure airway; ceftriaxone`],[`Diphtheria`,`Grey membrane bleeds on removal, bull neck`,`Antitoxin without waiting, erythromycin, notify`],[`Infectious mononucleosis`,`Membranous tonsils, posterior nodes, spleen`,`No amoxicillin; monospot; no contact sport`],[`Agranulocytosis`,`Fever + sore throat on carbimazole or clozapine`,`Stop drug, urgent CBC, febrile neutropenia care`]]}]},"eye-ent-rhinitis-sinusitis":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Allergic rhinitis** is IgE-mediated nasal inflammation: sneeze, itch, watery discharge, blockage.`,`**Itch** separates allergic rhinitis from a cold.`,`**Rhinosinusitis** is blockage or discharge plus facial pain or reduced smell.`,`Acute rhinosinusitis lasts under 12 weeks; **chronic** lasts 12 weeks or more.`,`Over 98% of acute rhinosinusitis is viral; only **0.5-2% of colds** become bacterial.`,`About 80% of asthmatics have rhinitis: **one airway, one disease**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**ARIA intermittent**: under 4 days a week or under 4 consecutive weeks.`,`**ARIA persistent**: 4 or more days a week and over 4 weeks.`,`**Moderate-severe** if any of sleep, school, work, sport or daily activity is impaired.`,`Acute rhinosinusitis is viral, post-viral or **acute bacterial (ABRS)**.`,`Chronic rhinosinusitis is with or without polyps, confirmed by **endoscopy or CT**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Inhaled allergen drives **Th2 cells** (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13), and B cells make IgE.`,`2. IgE binds to mast cells in the nasal mucosa.`,`3. Early phase: **histamine** causes itch, sneezing and watery discharge within minutes.`,`4. Late phase (4-8 hours): **eosinophils** cause persistent blockage and loss of smell.`,`5. Steroids switch off the late phase, so they best relieve blockage.`,`6. In sinusitis, mucosal swelling blocks the **ostiomeatal complex** and secretions stagnate.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask which symptom dominates: **sneezers-runners** versus **blockers**.`,`Ask about sleep, school or work days lost and sport to grade severity.`,`Screen for **asthma**: wheeze, night cough, exercise breathlessness.`,`Ask about decongestant drops used for more than 5-7 days.`,`Sinusitis: over 10 days, **double sickening**, or fever over 39 C suggests bacteria.`,`Any **unilateral** blockage, bloody discharge or facial numbness is a red flag.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for allergic shiners, transverse nasal crease and adenoid facies.`,`Allergic inferior turbinates are **pale, boggy and bluish**.`,`A polyp is **pale, grape-like, insensitive** and does not bleed; a turbinate is sensitive.`,`A unilateral, red or bleeding mass is not an allergic polyp.`,`In every sinusitis check **eye movements, acuity and proptosis**.`,`Look at the palate for a **black eschar** in diabetics.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Allergic rhinitis is a clinical diagnosis**; start treatment without tests.`,`Skin prick test or specific IgE only before **immunotherapy** or if diagnosis unclear.`,`Total IgE and eosinophil count do not diagnose allergic rhinitis.`,`**Acute sinusitis needs no X-ray**; Waters view does not change management.`,`**Contrast CT** for orbital or intracranial complications or unilateral disease.`,`Suspected mucormycosis: blood sugar, ketones and urgent **KOH and biopsy**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Allergen avoidance: mattress covers, **wash bedding weekly at 60 C**, avoid smoke.`,`Saline spray or irrigation with boiled-and-cooled or bottled water.`,`Mild intermittent: oral or intranasal **antihistamine as needed**.`,`Moderate-severe or persistent: **intranasal corticosteroid**, preferred over oral antihistamines (ARIA 2025).`,`Steroid sprays take **1-2 weeks**; aim away from the septum.`,`Viral sinusitis: paracetamol, fluids, steam and saline; lasts **7-10 days**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Severe persistent: add **azelastine-fluticasone** combination spray.`,`Montelukast only as an add-on when **asthma coexists**.`,`**Allergen immunotherapy for 3 years** is the only disease-modifying treatment.`,`ABRS: **amoxicillin or co-amoxiclav for 5-7 days** in adults.`,`Penicillin allergy: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily**; azithromycin is a poor choice.`,`CRS with polyps: INCS, short oral steroid courses, FESS, then biologics.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Orbital cellulitis**: proptosis, painful restricted eye movements, diplopia; admit for IV antibiotics and CT.`,`Preseptal cellulitis has lid swelling with normal vision and free eye movements.`,`**Mucormycosis**: diabetic or steroid-treated with black eschar; debridement and amphotericin B.`,`Pott puffy tumour is boggy forehead swelling from frontal osteomyelitis.`,`Decongestant drops beyond **5 days** cause rhinitis medicamentosa.`,`Never give **depot steroid injections** for hay fever.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review allergic rhinitis at **2-4 weeks**; check spray technique before adding drugs.`,`Refer for immunotherapy if moderate-severe despite steroid plus antihistamine.`,`Refer within 2 weeks for **unilateral** blockage, bloody discharge, mass or loose teeth.`,`Unilateral foul discharge in a child is a **foreign body** until removed.`,`Reassess ABRS at 3-7 days and reconsider dental source or polyps.`]}],tables:[{heading:`ARIA classification and first-line treatment`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`First-line treatment`],rows:[[`Mild intermittent`,`Under 4 days/week or 4 weeks; no impairment`,`Antihistamine as needed, saline`],[`Moderate-severe intermittent`,`Under 4 days/week or 4 weeks; impaired`,`Intranasal steroid`],[`Mild persistent`,`4+ days/week and over 4 weeks; no impairment`,`Intranasal steroid or regular antihistamine`],[`Moderate-severe persistent`,`4+ days/week and over 4 weeks; impaired`,`Steroid plus azelastine spray; consider immunotherapy`]]},{heading:`Drugs in rhinitis and sinusitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Mometasone nasal spray 50 mcg`,`2 sprays each nostril once daily (3-11 y: 1)`,`Continuous in persistent AR`,`Dryness, epistaxis, rare septal perforation`],[`Fluticasone propionate spray 50 mcg`,`2 sprays each nostril daily, then 1`,`Continuous; full effect 1-2 weeks`,`Epistaxis, crusting`],[`Azelastine-fluticasone spray`,`1 spray each nostril twice daily (12 y+)`,`Persistent severe AR`,`Bitter taste, mild sedation`],[`Levocetirizine`,`5 mg oral once daily, at night`,`As needed or seasonal`,`Mild sedation; halve in renal impairment`],[`Fexofenadine`,`120-180 mg oral once daily`,`As needed or seasonal`,`Not with fruit juice or antacids`],[`Montelukast`,`10 mg at night (6-14 y: 5 mg)`,`Add-on with asthma`,`Boxed warning: nightmares, mood change`],[`Oxymetazoline 0.05% drops`,`2-3 drops each nostril twice daily`,`Maximum 5 days`,`Rhinitis medicamentosa; not under 2 years`],[`Amoxicillin / co-amoxiclav`,`500 mg TDS / 625 mg TDS oral`,`5-7 days adult; child 10-14 days`,`Diarrhoea, rash; ABRS criteria only`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral twice daily`,`5-7 days`,`Not in pregnancy or under 8 years`]]},{heading:`Preseptal versus orbital cellulitis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Preseptal`,`Orbital`],rows:[[`Lid swelling`,`Present`,`Present`],[`Visual acuity`,`Normal`,`May be reduced, RAPD`],[`Eye movements`,`Full, painless`,`Painful, restricted, diplopia`],[`Proptosis, chemosis`,`Absent`,`Present`],[`Management`,`Oral co-amoxiclav, review 24 h`,`Admit, IV ceftriaxone plus metronidazole, CT`]]}],remember:[`Itch means allergy; blockage means the patient needs a steroid spray.`,`Check spray technique before changing the drug.`,`Spray with the **opposite hand**, aim at the outer eye, away from the septum.`,`Colour of discharge does not mean bacteria; the **time course** does.`,`Decongestant drops beyond 5 days cause rebound blockage.`,`Any **unilateral nasal sign**: tumour in adults, foreign body in children.`,`Lid swelling with painful eye movement means orbital cellulitis - admit.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`ARIA 2025 (ARIA-EAACI 2024-2025 revision): INCS strongly recommended over oral H1-antihistamines; conditional recommendation for INCS plus intranasal antihistamine over INCS alone when monotherapy is insufficient`,`ARIA 2025: azelastine-fluticasone preferred over olopatadine-mometasone in adults; either in children; fluticasone furoate or propionate conditionally favoured among INCS`,`ARIA 2025: intranasal decongestants - suggest against long-term (over 5 days) use and against routinely adding to an INCS (reversal of 2010/2016); regular oral decongestants suggested against`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Allergic rhinitis and sinusitis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**AR**: IgE-mediated nasal inflammation with 2 or more of watery rhinorrhoea, sneezing, blockage, **itch** over 1 h on most days. **Rhinosinusitis**: blockage or discharge + facial pain or reduced smell (cough in children); **acute under 12 weeks, chronic 12 weeks or more** (EPOS 2020).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ARIA**: intermittent (under 4 days/week or under 4 weeks) vs persistent; **mild vs moderate-severe** (sleep, school, work, sport impaired). ARS: viral (under 10 days), post-viral, **ABRS** (over 10 days, double sickening, fever over 39 C + pus 3-4 days). CRS with/without polyps.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`House dust mite, cockroach, moulds, pets, pollens (Parthenium, Prosopis); atopy; smoke and biomass; occupational dusts. ABRS: **pneumococcus, H. influenzae, Moraxella**; dental roots (2nd premolar, 1st molar). Mucormycosis: **DKA, steroids, post-COVID**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Allergen -> dendritic cells -> **Th2 (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13) -> IgE** on mast cells -> early phase histamine (itch, sneeze, rhinorrhoea) -> late phase eosinophils (**blockage, priming**). Sinus: viral oedema -> **OMC block** -> stasis -> bacterial superinfection (0.5-2% of colds).`],[`Key history`,`Dominant symptom (sneezer-runner vs blocker), pattern (perennial/seasonal/occupational), **impact on sleep and school**, asthma screen, **decongestant use over 5-7 days**, aspirin sensitivity, sinusitis time course, **unilateral symptoms**, eye and brain symptoms, diabetes/steroids.`],[`Key examination`,`Allergic shiners, nasal crease, adenoid facies; anterior rhinoscopy (pale boggy turbinates, polyp vs turbinate - polyp insensitive, grape-like); middle meatal pus; **eyes in every sinusitis** (proptosis, movements, acuity, RAPD); palatal eschar; peak flow; level II nodes.`],[`Investigations`,`AR - **clinical**; **skin prick or specific IgE** only if uncertain or before immunotherapy; **no total IgE/AEC**. ARS - **no imaging**; **contrast CT** for complications, unilateral disease or failed therapy; endoscopy for CRS; KOH + biopsy for mucormycosis; beta-2 transferrin for CSF leak.`],[`Treatment - first line (AR)`,`Avoidance + saline. Mild intermittent: **oral non-sedating antihistamine as needed** (levocetirizine 5 mg, fexofenadine 120-180 mg). Persistent/moderate-severe: **INCS - strongly preferred over oral antihistamine (ARIA 2025)**: mometasone or fluticasone 2 sprays each nostril OD, full effect 1-2 weeks.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**INCS + intranasal antihistamine (fixed azelastine-fluticasone 1 spray BD)** - conditional over INCS alone (ARIA 2025); montelukast 10 mg only with asthma (neuropsychiatric warning); ipratropium for watery rhinorrhoea; **allergen immunotherapy 3 years** (only disease-modifying). No depot steroid.`],[`Treatment - decongestants (ARIA 2025)`,`Oxymetazoline/xylometazoline **maximum 5 days** (rhinitis medicamentosa); **suggest against long-term use and routine add-on to INCS**; brief course only while INCS starts; **no regular oral decongestants** (BP, glaucoma, prostatism, under 6).`],[`Treatment - sinusitis`,`Viral/post-viral: saline, paracetamol, **mometasone 200 microgram BD x 14 days** if over 10 days. **ABRS**: watchful waiting acceptable; **amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS x 5-7 days**; child amoxicillin 45-90 mg/kg/day 10-14 days; allergy doxycycline 100 mg BD. CRSwNP: INCS, short prednisolone, FESS, biologics.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Spray: blow nose, prime, **head slightly forward, opposite hand, aim to outer eye away from septum**, gentle breath, no hard sniff. Polyps: **head-down drops**. Irrigation: **boiled-cooled or bottled water**, 150-240 mL per side; saline first, then steroid.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy: **budesonide INCS**, loratadine or cetirizine, saline. Children: mometasone from 3 y, fluticasone furoate from 6 y, check height yearly; no first-generation antihistamines. AERD: avoid NSAIDs. Diabetics on steroids: mucormycosis watch.`],[`Complications`,`Asthma (united airway), OME, sleep disturbance, CRS/polyps; sinusitis -> **preseptal/orbital cellulitis (Chandler I-V)**, cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, frontal abscess, **Pott puffy tumour**; mucormycosis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Proptosis, painful restricted eye movement, diplopia, falling vision** - admit, IV ceftriaxone + metronidazole, CT; black eschar in diabetic - same-day debridement + **liposomal amphotericin 5 mg/kg/day**; **unilateral blockage, blood-stained discharge, mass - urgent ENT** (tumour).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review AR at **2-4 weeks** - check **spray technique and adherence before adding a drug**; step down off-season; record sleep, school days, asthma control, epistaxis, child height. ABRS reassess 3-7 days.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ARIA 2025**: INCS over oral antihistamine (strong); INAH + INCS over INCS alone (conditional); azelastine-fluticasone preferred in adults; decongestants - against long-term and routine add-on.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Itch separates AR from a cold; **blockers need INCS**; check technique before changing drug; colour of discharge does not mean bacteria - time course does; any unilateral nasal sign = tumour (adult) or foreign body (child); one airway, one disease.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Allergic rhinitis ladder (ARIA 2025)`,caption:`Check technique and adherence before every step up; review 2-4 weeks.`,steps:[{label:`Avoidance and saline irrigation`,detail:`Mite covers, wash bedding at 60 C; saline 5-10 mL per nostril OD-BD`,tone:`good`},{label:`Mild intermittent: oral antihistamine as needed`,detail:`Levocetirizine 5 mg or fexofenadine 120-180 mg; never chlorpheniramine`},{label:`Persistent or moderate-severe: INCS daily`,detail:`Strongly preferred over oral antihistamine; 2 sprays each nostril OD; 1-2 weeks`},{label:`Not controlled: add intranasal antihistamine`,detail:`Fixed azelastine-fluticasone 1 spray each nostril BD (12 y+)`},{label:`Still uncontrolled: allergy referral`,detail:`Skin prick test, allergen immunotherapy SCIT/SLIT for 3 years`},{label:`Decongestant only briefly`,detail:`Max 5 days while INCS starts; never long term; no depot steroid`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute rhinosinusitis - antibiotic or not`,steps:[{label:`Nasal blockage/discharge + facial pain or smell loss`,detail:`Viral in over 98%; no X-ray`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any orbital or intracranial sign?`,detail:`Proptosis, painful eye movement, diplopia, headache, meningism - admit, CT`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Under 10 days and improving: viral`,detail:`Saline, paracetamol, fluids; colour of mucus irrelevant`},{label:`Worse after day 5 or over 10 days: post-viral`,detail:`No ABRS features: mometasone 200 microgram BD x 14 days`},{label:`ABRS criteria met: over 10 days, double sickening, severe`,detail:`Severe = fever 39 C + pus 3-4 days; amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS 5-7 days`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Reassess at 3-7 days`,detail:`Failure: high-dose co-amoxiclav or doxycycline; think dental, polyp, tumour`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Preseptal versus orbital cellulitis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Preseptal`,`Orbital`],rows:[[`Vision and colour`,`Normal`,`Reduced; RAPD possible`],[`Eye movements`,`Full, painless`,`Painful, restricted; diplopia`],[`Proptosis, chemosis`,`Absent`,`Present`],[`Management`,`Oral co-amoxiclav, review 24 h`,`Admit, IV ceftriaxone + metronidazole, contrast CT, drain abscess`]]}]},"eye-ent-refractive-error":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Refractive error** is failure to focus parallel light on the retina with accommodation relaxed.`,`It is the **leading cause of visual impairment** worldwide and fully correctable.`,`**Presenting acuity** is acuity with whatever glasses the patient walks in with.`,`India defines **blindness as presenting acuity worse than 3/60** in the better eye since 2017.`,`**Near vision impairment** is worse than N6 at 40 cm with existing correction.`,`**Amblyopia** is reduced best-corrected acuity from abnormal visual development in childhood.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Myopia** focuses in front of the retina; corrected by a **concave (minus) lens**.`,`High myopia is **-6.00 D or more** or axial length over 26 mm.`,`**Hypermetropia** focuses behind the retina; corrected by a **convex (plus) lens**.`,`**Astigmatism** has no single focus; regular needs a cylinder, irregular a rigid lens.`,`**Presbyopia** is age-related loss of accommodation from about **40 years**.`,`**Anisometropia** is a difference of 2 D or more between the eyes.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The cornea gives about 43 D and the lens about 17-20 D of power.`,`2. Each **1 mm change in axial length** alters refraction by about 3 D.`,`3. Childhood myopia is mostly **axial**: the eye grows too long.`,`4. Bright outdoor light releases **retinal dopamine**, which slows eye elongation.`,`5. Hypermetropes over-accommodate, which drives convergence and **accommodative esotropia**.`,`6. In presbyopia the lens hardens, so the near point recedes beyond reading distance.`,`7. In amblyopia the brain **suppresses the blurred eye** during the critical period.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask whether **distance, near or both** are blurred.`,`**Sudden loss of vision** is never a refraction problem.`,`Frontal headache after reading, absent on waking, suggests uncorrected error.`,`Children: sitting close to the board, squinting, head tilt, falling school marks.`,`Ask about **daily outdoor time** and screen time in children.`,`Ask about flashes, floaters or a curtain, especially in **high myopes**.`,`Ask about diabetes and **topiramate**; both can shift refraction.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Snellen chart at **6 metres**, one eye at a time, first with existing glasses.`,`**Pinhole**: improvement means refractive error; no improvement means refer.`,`Near acuity with an **N-notation chart at 40 cm**.`,`Children 3-5 years: **Tumbling E, Lea symbols** or matching tests.`,`**Bruckner red reflex test**: dull, white or unequal reflex needs referral.`,`Hirschberg and **cover-uncover test** for squint.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Refraction** by optometrist or ophthalmologist; autorefraction alone is not a prescription.`,`**Cycloplegic refraction is mandatory in children**, since accommodation hides hypermetropia.`,`Dilated fundus **every year in high myopia**.`,`IOP and disc check from 40 years and in high myopia.`,`Keratometry or topography for increasing astigmatism to detect **keratoconus**.`,`Check sugar if refraction fluctuates; defer refraction until glucose is stable.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Spectacles are the mainstay**: cheap, safe and reversible.`,`Ready-made readers are fine for presbyopia with equal eyes and little astigmatism.`,`**Glasses do not weaken the eyes**, and under-correction does not slow myopia.`,`Myopia prevention: **2 hours outdoors daily**, breaks from near work, less screen time.`,`**20-20-20 rule** for screen users relieves strain but does not change refraction.`,`Amblyopia: full-time glasses first, then **patching or atropine penalisation**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Progressive childhood myopia: **atropine 0.01-0.05%** at bedtime for at least 2 years.`,`**0.05% is the most effective** low-dose atropine; taper rather than stop.`,`Optical myopia control: defocus spectacles, dual-focus lenses or **orthokeratology**.`,`Patching: **2 hours daily for moderate**, 6 hours for severe amblyopia.`,`Refractive surgery needs age over 18 and **stable refraction for a year**.`,`Low vision aids when best-corrected acuity stays poor.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`High myopia brings **retinal detachment**, myopic maculopathy and glaucoma.`,`**Leukocoria** in a child means retinoblastoma or cataract until proved otherwise.`,`Topiramate with sudden bilateral blur and pain: **myopic shift and angle closure**.`,`Red painful eye in a contact lens wearer: microbial keratitis; same-day referral.`,`Cyclopentolate can cause **hallucinations and ataxia** in children.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NPCB began in 1976**; renamed NPCBVI in 2017.`,`NPCBVI gives **free school spectacles** and free presbyopic glasses for the elderly.`,`Teachers screen schoolchildren with a **6/9 cut-off**.`,`**RBSK** screens children 0-18 years for the 4Ds.`,`**Vitamin A**: 100,000 IU at 9 months, then 200,000 IU 6-monthly to 5 years (9 doses).`,`Refer children with acuity worse than 6/9 or a **two-line difference** between eyes.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of refractive error`,columns:[`Type`,`Where light focuses`,`Correction`,`Key symptom`],rows:[[`Myopia`,`In front of retina`,`Concave (minus) lens`,`Distance blur`],[`Hypermetropia`,`Behind retina`,`Convex (plus) lens`,`Near strain, esotropia in children`],[`Astigmatism`,`No single point`,`Cylinder; rigid lens if irregular`,`Blur at all distances`],[`Presbyopia`,`Near point recedes`,`Convex near addition`,`Near blur after 40`]]},{heading:`WHO visual impairment categories (better eye)`,columns:[`Category`,`Presenting acuity`,`Action`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Worse than 6/12 to 6/18`,`Pinhole; refract`],[`Moderate`,`Worse than 6/18 to 6/60`,`Refract; find cause`],[`Severe`,`Worse than 6/60 to 3/60`,`Refer; low vision services`],[`Blindness`,`Worse than 3/60 or field under 10 degrees`,`Refer; disability certificate`],[`Near impairment`,`Worse than N6 at 40 cm`,`Near addition`]]},{heading:`Drugs in refraction and myopia control`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cyclopentolate 1% (0.5% infants)`,`1 drop, repeat after 5-10 min`,`Refract at 30-45 min; lasts 24 h`,`Hallucinations, ataxia in children`],[`Atropine 1% ointment`,`Twice daily for 3 days before refraction`,`Effect lasts 7-14 days`,`Fever, flushing, delirium`],[`Atropine 0.01-0.05% drops`,`1 drop each eye at bedtime`,`At least 2 years`,`Photophobia, near blur; taper`],[`Atropine 1% (penalisation)`,`1 drop in better eye daily or weekends`,`Under ophthalmologist`,`Photophobia, systemic effects`],[`Tropicamide 1% + phenylephrine 2.5%`,`1 drop, repeat after 5 min`,`Dilation lasts 4-6 h`,`No driving; avoid if shallow chamber`],[`Carboxymethylcellulose 0.5%`,`1 drop 3-4 times daily`,`As needed`,`Transient blur`],[`Vitamin A`,`100,000 IU at 9 months, then 200,000 IU`,`6-monthly to 5 years`,`Raised ICP in overdose`]]}],remember:[`The **pinhole** is the cheapest test: improves means refractive, no improvement means refer.`,`Never refract a child **without cycloplegia**.`,`A child never complains of one bad eye - amblyopia is found by screening.`,`Treat amblyopia **before 7-8 years**.`,`Two hours outdoors delays myopia; low-dose atropine slows it.`,`**Second sight** in the elderly is nuclear cataract, not recovery.`,`India: blindness is presenting acuity **worse than 3/60** (since 2017).`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`IMI 2025 Digest: delaying myopia onset in pre-myopic children made a priority (each year of delay worth about 2-3 years of progression control); outdoor time remains the strongest protective factor`,`EGS 2025 (6th edition) replaces EGS 2020 for the glaucoma check from 40 years`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Refractive error, presbyopia and NPCBVI`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Failure to focus parallel rays on the retina with accommodation relaxed - **axial length vs refractive power mismatch**; **uncorrected refractive error is the leading cause of visual impairment** and fully correctable.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Myopia** (minus lens; high = -6 D or more or axial length over 26 mm), **hypermetropia** (plus lens), **astigmatism** (cylinder; irregular - rigid lens), **presbyopia** (near add +1.00 D at 40-45 to +2.50-3.00 after 60), **anisometropia 2 D or more**. WHO: blindness **presenting acuity worse than 3/60** (India since 2017); near impairment worse than N6.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Myopia: **near work, under 2 h outdoors**, parental myopia, urban schooling. Hypermetropia: short eye -> accommodative esotropia. Keratoconus: rubbing, allergy. Drugs: **topiramate** (myopic shift + angle closure); diabetes (glucose swings).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Peripheral hyperopic defocus -> **scleral remodelling -> axial elongation** (1 mm = about 3 D); bright light -> **retinal dopamine** inhibits growth; atropine acts on retinal/scleral muscarinic receptors. Amblyopia: blurred/deviated eye suppressed in **critical period to 7-8 years**.`],[`Key history`,`Distance vs near blur, gradual vs **sudden (never refractive)**, strain/frontal headache after reading, child sitting close to board, squint, family history, **screen and outdoor time**, flashes/floaters in myopes, diabetes, drugs, existing glasses, occupation and driving.`],[`Key examination`,`**Snellen at 6 m, each eye, with own glasses (presenting acuity)**; **pinhole** (improves = refractive); near N chart at 40 cm; children - Lea, tumbling E; **Bruckner red reflex**, Hirschberg, cover test; RAPD; torch and fundus.`],[`Investigations`,`Refraction (retinoscopy + subjective); **cycloplegic refraction mandatory in children**; dilated fundus yearly in high myopia; IOP/disc from 40; topography for keratoconus; **axial length 6-12 monthly** in myopia control; HbA1c if refraction fluctuates.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Spectacles** (worn constantly); ready-made readers for presbyopia with equal eyes; contact lenses with strict hygiene; myopia prevention **2 h outdoors daily**, near-work breaks, 20-20-20 rule.`],[`Treatment - myopia control`,`**Atropine 0.01-0.05% 1 drop at bedtime for at least 2 years** (0.05% most effective), review 6-monthly, **taper not stop**; **defocus spectacles (DIMS/HAL), dual-focus soft lenses, orthokeratology** slow progression 30-60%; under-correction does not help.`],[`Treatment - amblyopia`,`Full-time glasses first (16-18 weeks), then **patching 2 h/day moderate, 6 h/day severe** or **atropine 1% penalisation** of better eye; must start **before 7-8 years**.`],[`Treatment - cycloplegics`,`**Cyclopentolate 1% (0.5% infants) 1 drop x 2, refract at 30-45 min**; atropine 1% ointment BD x 3 days before refraction (7-14 days effect); toxicity - hallucinations, ataxia, fever; **punctal pressure**; tropicamide 1% for adult fundus.`],[`Special groups`,`Preterm under 34 weeks or 2000 g - **ROP screen by 4 weeks or 31 weeks PMA, whichever later**; diabetic - defer refraction until glucose stable; elderly - avoid bifocals on stairs (falls); topiramate users - sudden blur + pain = emergency.`],[`Complications`,`Amblyopia, strabismus, **retinal detachment, myopic maculopathy, glaucoma** in high myopia, keratitis from lens misuse, school failure, falls.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Leukocoria/absent red reflex** (same week), sudden vision loss, flashes + curtain, no pinhole improvement, new squint/diplopia, two-line acuity difference in child, red painful lens-wearer eye, topiramate blur.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Children with glasses **6-12 monthly** (6-monthly on myopia control); adults every 2 years; high myopes yearly dilated fundus; amblyopia to equal or best acuity then maintenance.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NPCBVI** (1976; renamed 2017): free cataract surgery, **school screening (6/9 cut-off) with free spectacles**, free presbyopic spectacles, Vision Centres, eye donation; goal **blindness 0.25% by 2025** (last survey 0.36%); RBSK 4Ds; vitamin A 1 lakh IU at 9 months then 2 lakh 6-monthly to 5 years.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IMI 2025**: prevent or delay myopia onset in pre-myopes - outdoor time strongest, low-dose atropine, red-light therapy and novel spectacles emerging.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Presenting acuity reveals uncorrected error; **pinhole improvement = refractive**; never refract a child without cycloplegia; 2 h outdoors delays onset; atropine 0.05% slows progression; second sight = nuclear cataract; glasses do not weaken eyes.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Blurred vision in the OPD - is it refractive?`,steps:[{label:`Sudden loss or pain?`,detail:`Yes = not refractive; vascular, detachment, optic neuritis - refer same day`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Presenting acuity each eye at 6 m`,detail:`With own glasses; then near N chart at 40 cm`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pinhole improves acuity?`,detail:`Yes = refractive error; no = media, retina or optic nerve - refer`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Child: cycloplegic refraction`,detail:`Cyclopentolate 1% (0.5% infant); check red reflex and squint`},{label:`Adult over 40 with near blur`,detail:`Presbyopia - readers; check IOP and disc`},{label:`Prescribe and link to free spectacles`,detail:`NPCBVI school and presbyopia schemes; review 6-24 months`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Childhood myopia - prevent, then control`,steps:[{label:`Pre-myopia: delay onset (IMI 2025)`,detail:`2 h outdoors daily, near-work breaks, reading over 30 cm`,tone:`good`},{label:`Full single-vision correction`,detail:`Under-correction does not slow myopia`},{label:`Progressing 0.5 D or more a year: low-dose atropine`,detail:`0.01-0.05% at bedtime at least 2 years; 0.05% most effective`},{label:`Optical control`,detail:`DIMS/HAL defocus spectacles, dual-focus soft lenses, ortho-k`},{label:`Combine and monitor axial length`,detail:`6-monthly refraction and biometry; taper atropine on stopping`,tone:`decision`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`WHO visual impairment by presenting acuity (better eye)`,columns:[`Category`,`Acuity`,`Action`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Worse than 6/12 to 6/18`,`Pinhole, refraction`],[`Moderate`,`Worse than 6/18 to 6/60`,`Refract, find cause`],[`Severe`,`Worse than 6/60 to 3/60`,`Refer, low vision`],[`Blindness`,`Worse than 3/60 or field under 10 degrees`,`Refer, disability certificate`],[`Near impairment`,`Worse than N6 at 40 cm`,`Near addition`]]}]},"eye-ent-cataract-dr-glaucoma":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The family physician's job in these three diseases is to **detect and refer**, not treat.`,`**Cataract** is any opacity of the lens; it causes about **66% of blindness** in Indians over 50.`,`**Diabetic retinopathy** is microvascular retinal damage from chronic hyperglycaemia.`,`**Glaucoma** is a progressive optic neuropathy with cupping and matching field loss.`,`Raised IOP is the main modifiable risk, but **not required** for diagnosis.`,`Over **90% of glaucoma** in India is undiagnosed.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Cataract by morphology: **nuclear, cortical, posterior subcapsular**.`,`DR grades: no DR, mild, moderate, severe NPDR, and **proliferative DR**.`,`**Severe NPDR 4-2-1 rule**: haemorrhages in 4 quadrants, venous beading in 2, IRMA in 1.`,`Macular oedema is graded separately; **centre-involving** needs urgent treatment.`,`Glaucoma is **open-angle** or **angle-closure**, decided by gonioscopy.`,`**Ocular hypertension**: IOP over 21 mmHg with normal disc, field and open angle.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Cataract: oxidative damage makes **crystallin proteins** clump and scatter light.`,`2. Glaucoma: pressure bows the **lamina cribrosa** and blocks axoplasmic flow.`,`3. Ganglion cells die, so the cup enlarges and the rim thins.`,`4. Field loss starts with **arcuate scotomas and a nasal step**; central vision survives till late.`,`5. DR: **pericyte loss** weakens capillaries, causing microaneurysms, the first sign.`,`6. Leaking vessels cause **macular oedema** and hard exudates.`,`7. Ischaemic retina releases **VEGF**, which drives new vessels that bleed and pull.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Cataract: gradual painless blur, **glare** around headlights, faded colours.`,`**Second sight** (reading without glasses) suggests nuclear cataract.`,`Sudden or painful loss of vision is **never cataract**.`,`Ask **how vision limits daily life**: reading, faces, cooking, driving, falls.`,`Ask diabetics the date and result of the **last retinal screen**.`,`Glaucoma risks: over 40, **first-degree family history**, myopia, diabetes, steroids.`,`Ask about **tamsulosin** before cataract surgery (floppy iris).`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Acuity with **pinhole**: no improvement means lens, retina or nerve disease.`,`Red reflex: lens opacities show as **black shadows**; white reflex in a child is retinoblastoma.`,`Do the **oblique torch shadow test** before dilating any pupil.`,`Dilate with **tropicamide 1%**; it acts in 20-30 minutes.`,`Disc: **cup-disc ratio 0.6 or more** or asymmetry over 0.2 is suspicious.`,`Look for disc haemorrhage and rim notching against the **ISNT rule**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`DR screening needs **dilated fundus exam or retinal photography**.`,`**Undilated direct ophthalmoscopy** is not an adequate DR screen.`,`Diabetics also need HbA1c, lipids, eGFR, urine ACR and haemoglobin.`,`Glaucoma workup: tonometry, pachymetry, **gonioscopy**, OCT and automated perimetry.`,`Before cataract surgery: biometry, sugar and BP; routine ECG and X-ray are not needed.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Cataract has no medical treatment**; surgery when vision limits daily life.`,`Surgery is phaco or **MSICS with IOL**; reading glasses are still needed.`,`DR at every stage: control HbA1c, **BP under 130/80**, statin, stop smoking.`,`Glaucoma: lower IOP by **20-30%** to a target pressure.`,`**Latanoprost 0.005%** once at night is the first-line drop.`,`**SLT laser** is now first-line on a par with prostaglandin drops.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Add or switch drops: **timolol**, brimonidine, dorzolamide or fixed combinations.`,`Angle-closure disease: **laser peripheral iridotomy** in both eyes.`,`Centre-involving DME: **intravitreal anti-VEGF** injections.`,`PDR: **panretinal photocoagulation** or anti-VEGF; vitrectomy for non-clearing bleeds.`,`**Fenofibrate** may slow DR progression in type 2 diabetes.`,`Review retina before and after rapid HbA1c lowering with insulin or GLP-1 agonists.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Sudden painless loss, floaters or a curtain: detachment or vitreous haemorrhage; same day.`,`Painful red eye with a **hypermature cataract** means lens-induced glaucoma.`,`Pain and falling vision within 6 weeks of surgery is **endophthalmitis**.`,`**Timolol** can cause fatal bronchospasm; ask about asthma and check pulse.`,`Posterior capsular opacification in **10-30%**, treated by Nd:YAG capsulotomy.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Screen **type 2 at diagnosis**, type 1 at 5 years, then yearly.`,`Pregnancy with pre-existing diabetes: screen each trimester; **gestational diabetes needs no screen**.`,`Refer glaucoma suspects within weeks; check IOP **4-6 weeks** after a new drop.`,`Screen **first-degree relatives** of glaucoma patients from 40 years.`,`**NPCBVI** gives free cataract surgery and DR and glaucoma screening.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Diabetic retinopathy grades and action`,columns:[`Grade`,`Fundus findings`,`Action`],rows:[[`No DR`,`Normal`,`Rescreen in 12 months`],[`Mild NPDR`,`Microaneurysms only`,`Rescreen 6-12 months; tighten control`],[`Moderate NPDR`,`More than mild, less than severe`,`Ophthalmology within 3-6 months`],[`Severe NPDR`,`4-2-1 rule, no new vessels`,`Ophthalmology within weeks`],[`PDR`,`New vessels, vitreous haemorrhage`,`Urgent: PRP or anti-VEGF`],[`Centre-involving DME`,`Thickening or exudates at fovea`,`Urgent anti-VEGF`]]},{heading:`Drugs in glaucoma and DR`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Latanoprost 0.005%`,`1 drop once daily at night`,`Lifelong`,`Red eye, iris darkening, lash growth`],[`Timolol 0.5%`,`1 drop twice daily`,`Lifelong`,`Bronchospasm, bradycardia; not in asthma, COPD, block`],[`Brimonidine 0.2%`,`1 drop 2-3 times daily`,`Lifelong`,`Allergy, dry mouth; not under 2 years`],[`Dorzolamide 2%`,`1 drop three times daily`,`Lifelong`,`Stinging, bitter taste; sulpha allergy`],[`Acetazolamide`,`250 mg oral 6-8 hourly`,`Short term only`,`Hypokalaemia, acidosis, stones`],[`Anti-VEGF (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab)`,`Intravitreal, monthly loading`,`Then treat-and-extend`,`Endophthalmitis, IOP rise`],[`Prednisolone acetate 1% (post-cataract)`,`4 times daily`,`Taper over 4-6 weeks`,`IOP rise; never stop abruptly`],[`Fenofibrate`,`145-160 mg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`Adjust for renal function`]]},{heading:`Cataract types and symptoms`,columns:[`Type`,`Typical symptom`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Nuclear`,`Poor distance vision, second sight`,`Index myopia, frequent glasses change`],[`Posterior subcapsular`,`Glare and near blur in bright light`,`Steroids, diabetes`],[`Cortical`,`Glare from oncoming headlights`,`Spoke-like opacities`],[`Intumescent / hypermature`,`Painful red eye`,`Lens-induced glaucoma; urgent surgery`]]}],remember:[`Refer cataract when it **limits the patient's life**, not at a fixed Snellen line.`,`Half of glaucoma patients have a **normal single IOP** - disc and field diagnose it.`,`Glaucoma drops prevent further loss but **never restore vision**.`,`Screen type 2 at diagnosis, type 1 at 5 years; gestational diabetes needs no screen.`,`Macular oedema can occur at **any grade** of retinopathy.`,`Check chamber depth before dilating any pupil.`,`Tell the surgeon about tamsulosin - floppy iris.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`EGS 2025 (6th edition) and AAO POAG PPP 2026: SLT is a first-line option on a par with prostaglandin drops in POAG and ocular hypertension; trabeculectomy remains the benchmark for low targets`,`Citations moved from EGS 2020 (5th edition) to EGS 2025 (6th edition); AAO POAG PPP 2026 replaces 2020`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Cataract, diabetic retinopathy and glaucoma`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Cataract**: any lens opacity (about **66% of blindness at 50+**, NBVIS 2015-19). **DR**: microvascular retinal damage from hyperglycaemia. **Glaucoma**: progressive optic neuropathy with cupping and matching field loss; **IOP is the main modifiable risk, not a criterion**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Cataract: nuclear, cortical, posterior subcapsular; immature to hypermature. **DR International scale**: none, mild/moderate/severe NPDR (**4-2-1 rule**), PDR + DME (centre-involving or not). Glaucoma: **POAG (incl. NTG) vs primary angle-closure disease**, secondary (steroid, neovascular, lens-induced) - by gonioscopy; OHT over 21 mmHg.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Cataract: age, UV, smoking, **diabetes, steroids**. DR: **duration, HbA1c, BP**, lipids, nephropathy, anaemia, pregnancy, puberty, rapid HbA1c fall. Glaucoma: **age over 40, first-degree relative (4-10x)**, myopia (POAG), hypermetropia (angle closure), steroids, diabetes.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Lens: oxidative crystallin aggregation; diabetic **sorbitol** osmotic swelling. DR: hyperglycaemia -> **pericyte loss** -> microaneurysms, leakage (DME) -> capillary closure -> **VEGF** -> new vessels -> haemorrhage, traction. Glaucoma: IOP/lamina strain -> **axoplasmic block -> ganglion apoptosis** -> superior/inferior rim loss -> arcuate scotoma, nasal step.`],[`Key history`,`Gradual painless blur, glare, **second sight**, monocular diplopia; **function (reading, faces, driving, falls)**; diabetes duration and **last retinal screen**; floaters/curtain; glaucoma family history, steroid use (incl. creams, inhalers); intermittent haloes; **tamsulosin** (floppy iris); asthma before timolol; leukocoria in child.`],[`Key examination`,`Acuity + **pinhole (no improvement)**; **red reflex** (black shadows on red); oblique shadow test before dilating; **tropicamide 1%** dilation; disc: **CDR 0.6 or more, asymmetry over 0.2**, ISNT rule, disc haemorrhage; DR lesions; confrontation fields; RAPD; BP, feet, urine albumin.`],[`Investigations`,`DR screen: **dilated fundus exam or retinal photography** (undilated direct ophthalmoscopy inadequate); HbA1c, lipids, eGFR, **UACR**, Hb. Glaucoma (eye team): applanation (10-21 mmHg), **pachymetry, gonioscopy**, OCT RNFL, **Humphrey 24-2**. Pre-cataract: biometry, sugar, BP - no routine ECG/CXR.`],[`DR screening schedule (ADA 2026)`,`**Type 2 at diagnosis; type 1 within 5 years**; then **yearly** (1-2 yearly if repeatedly normal and well controlled); **pregnancy with pre-existing diabetes** - pre-conception/first trimester, each trimester, 1 year postpartum; **GDM - no screen**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Cataract: surgery only** (phaco or MSICS + PCIOL) when vision limits daily life. **DR: systemic control** - individualised HbA1c about 7%, **BP under 130/80**, statin, stop smoking. **Glaucoma: target 20-30% IOP fall**; **latanoprost 0.005% at night** or **SLT first-line (EGS 2025, AAO 2026)**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Glaucoma: switch if under 15-20% fall at 4-6 weeks, or add **timolol 0.5% BD, brimonidine 0.2% BD-TDS, dorzolamide 2% TDS**, fixed combinations; laser/trabeculectomy after 2-3 drugs. **Angle closure: laser peripheral iridotomy** or lens extraction. DR: **anti-VEGF for centre-involving DME**; **PRP** for PDR; vitrectomy; fenofibrate may slow DR.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`One drop, close eye, **punctal occlusion 1-2 min** (cuts systemic timolol/brimonidine), **5 min between drops**, prostaglandin at bedtime, wipe lids, discard 4 weeks after opening; drop-guide aids; preservative-free if surface disease.`],[`Special groups`,`**Timolol contraindicated in asthma, COPD, bradycardia, heart block**; brimonidine under 2 years contraindicated; acetazolamide - avoid sulpha allergy, renal failure, sickle cell; pregnancy - avoid prostaglandins if possible, anti-VEGF avoided; **congenital cataract surgery within 6-8 weeks**.`],[`Complications`,`Cataract: phacomorphic/phacolytic glaucoma; post-op **endophthalmitis**, PCO (10-30%, YAG). DR: vitreous haemorrhage, tractional RD, **neovascular glaucoma**. Glaucoma: irreversible field loss; drug effects - iris darkening, bronchospasm, allergy.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Sudden painless loss/curtain - same day; **PDR or centre-involving DME - urgent**; severe NPDR within weeks; moderate NPDR 3-6 months; acute angle closure - treat and refer within hours; pain after surgery or injection - endophthalmitis; **leukocoria**; disc haemorrhage or cup asymmetry.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`IOP **4-6 weeks** after new drop, then 3-12 monthly; fields and OCT 6-12 monthly; ask adherence, technique, pulse, wheeze; retinal review before and 3-6 months after intensifying glucose control in moderate+ NPDR; post-cataract day 1, week 1, 4-6 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NPCBVI**: free cataract surgery with IOL, DR and glaucoma screening in Vision Centres, Ayushman Arogya Mandirs, tele-ophthalmology; **CSR target above 6000/million/year**; diabetic and glaucoma-family registers; examine first-degree relatives from 40.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**EGS 2025 (6th edition) and AAO POAG PPP 2026**: SLT first-line alongside prostaglandins (LiGHT); MIGS for selected patients; more intensive monitoring for fast progressors.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Refer cataract on function, not Snellen line; **half of POAG have normal single IOP**; glaucoma loss is permanent; undilated ophthalmoscopy is not DR screening; DME at any grade; timolol drops can kill an asthmatic; look at the chamber before dilating; tell surgeon about tamsulosin.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Diabetic retinopathy - screen, grade, act`,caption:`ADA 2026 schedule with AAO PPP referral intervals.`,steps:[{label:`Screen: type 2 at diagnosis, type 1 within 5 years`,detail:`Dilated exam or retinal photograph; not undilated direct ophthalmoscopy`},{label:`Grade by International scale`,detail:`None, mild, moderate, severe NPDR (4-2-1), PDR; DME separately`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No DR or mild NPDR`,detail:`Rescreen 12 months (1-2 years if repeatedly normal); mild 6-12 months`},{label:`Moderate NPDR`,detail:`Ophthalmology within 3-6 months; tighten HbA1c, BP, lipids`},{label:`Severe NPDR, PDR or centre-involving DME`,detail:`Urgent: PRP or anti-VEGF; vitrectomy if haemorrhage or traction`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Systemic control at every stage`,detail:`HbA1c about 7%, BP under 130/80, statin, UACR, Hb`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`POAG treatment ladder (EGS 2025)`,caption:`Target IOP set by the ophthalmologist; check response at 4-6 weeks.`,steps:[{label:`First line: prostaglandin or SLT`,detail:`Latanoprost 0.005% 1 drop at night (25-35% fall) or selective laser trabeculoplasty`,tone:`good`},{label:`Under 15-20% fall: switch, then add`,detail:`Timolol 0.5% BD, brimonidine 0.2%, dorzolamide 2% TDS`},{label:`Fixed combinations to cut drops`,detail:`Latanoprost-timolol OD, timolol-dorzolamide BD; preservative-free if surface disease`},{label:`Not at target on 2-3 drugs`,detail:`Repeat laser, MIGS in selected cases`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Advanced or fast progression`,detail:`Trabeculectomy - benchmark for low target pressures`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Cataract, DR and glaucoma at the bedside`,columns:[`Feature`,`Cataract`,`Diabetic retinopathy`,`POAG`],rows:[[`Symptom`,`Gradual blur, glare, second sight`,`Often none; distortion with DME`,`None until late field loss`],[`Key sign`,`Dull red reflex, no pinhole gain`,`Microaneurysms, exudates, new vessels`,`CDR 0.6+, asymmetry over 0.2, notch`],[`Primary care role`,`Refer on function`,`Screen yearly, control risk factors`,`Detect, refer, support adherence`],[`Definitive care`,`Phaco or MSICS + IOL`,`Anti-VEGF, PRP, vitrectomy`,`Drops or SLT, surgery`]]}]},"eye-ent-hearing-loss":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Conductive loss** arises between the pinna and the stapes footplate; maximum about **60 dB**.`,`**Sensorineural loss** arises in the cochlea or cochlear nerve and is usually permanent.`,`**Mixed loss** has both, as in long-standing CSOM or presbycusis with wax.`,`**Sudden SNHL**: loss of 30 dB or more over 3 frequencies within **72 hours**.`,`**Asymmetric SNHL** is a vestibular schwannoma until MRI proves otherwise.`,`WHO calls loss over **35 dB in the better ear** disabling.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Conductive: **wax**, OME, CSOM, otosclerosis and perforation.`,`Sensorineural: **presbycusis**, noise, ototoxic drugs, Meniere disease, schwannoma.`,`**Noise over 85 dB(A)** for 8 hours damages the cochlea.`,`Ototoxic drugs: **aminoglycosides**, cisplatin, loop diuretics, high-dose aspirin, quinine.`,`Congenital: **GJB2 (connexin 26)** is the commonest genetic cause; **CMV** the commonest non-genetic.`,`OME is the commonest acquired conductive cause in children.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The middle ear boosts sound pressure about 22 times, so its damage causes conductive loss.`,`2. The cochlear base detects **high frequencies**; noise, ageing and drugs hit it first.`,`3. **Outer hair cells** amplify soft sounds and produce otoacoustic emissions.`,`4. **Inner hair cells** transduce sound and carry about 95% of nerve fibres.`,`5. Noise damage produces a characteristic **4 kHz notch**.`,`6. Otosclerosis fixes the stapes footplate, giving a **Carhart notch at 2 kHz**.`,`7. Meniere disease is **endolymphatic hydrops** with vertigo, fluctuating loss and fullness.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Sudden onset over hours to 3 days means **refer today**.`,`Bilateral symmetrical loss fits presbycusis or noise; **unilateral needs explanation**.`,`Ask about tinnitus, vertigo, discharge and aural fullness.`,`Take a **noise history**: occupation, protection, earphones and firecrackers.`,`Ask about past **aminoglycoside** use, including TB injections.`,`In infants ask about NICU stay, jaundice, TORCH infection and speech milestones.`,`A **parent who thinks the child does not hear** is usually right.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Otoscopy after wax removal: perforation, effusion, cholesteatoma or normal drum.`,`Use a **512 Hz fork** struck on the elbow or knee.`,`**Rinne negative** (BC better than AC) means conductive loss of at least 15-20 dB.`,`**Weber** goes to the conductive ear and away from the sensorineural ear.`,`A **false negative Rinne** in a dead ear is exposed by Weber going to the good ear.`,`Check the **corneal reflex**; early loss suggests acoustic neuroma.`,`Examine the postnasal space in any **unilateral effusion in an adult**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Pure tone audiometry**: an air-bone gap over 10 dB means conductive loss.`,`Tympanometry: **type B** flat in effusion; type As in otosclerosis.`,`Newborns: **OAE** at the cot side, plus **automated ABR** for high-risk babies.`,`OAE misses auditory neuropathy, so high-risk babies need ABR.`,`**Gadolinium MRI** of the internal auditory meatus for asymmetric SNHL or SSNHL.`,`Child with SNHL: **CMV PCR** within 3 weeks, GJB2 testing and ECG for long QT.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Treat the cause: remove wax, manage OME and CSOM, offer stapedotomy or aid in otosclerosis.`,`**SSNHL**: prednisolone **1 mg/kg/day (max 60 mg)** for 7-14 days, then taper.`,`Start steroids **within 72 hours**; benefit is greatest in the first 2 weeks.`,`Hearing aids from **moderate loss (35 dB or more)**; binaural for bilateral loss.`,`Vitamins, vasodilators and nerve tonics **do not restore hearing**.`,`Families should speak clearly at normal volume and **not shout**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Failed oral steroid in SSNHL: **intratympanic steroid** within 2-6 weeks.`,`Meniere attacks: prochlorperazine or cinnarizine for **no more than 3 days**.`,`Meniere maintenance: salt restriction, **betahistine**, diuretic, then intratympanic treatment.`,`**Cochlear implant** for severe-to-profound loss with little benefit from aids.`,`Implant congenital deafness **by 12-24 months** for the best speech outcome.`,`Symptomatic congenital CMV: **valganciclovir** for 6 months, started in the first month.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Sudden loss with vertigo, facial weakness or ataxia may be **AICA stroke**.`,`Hearing loss after head injury with CSF in the canal: **temporal bone fracture**; no syringing.`,`New tinnitus during aminoglycoside or cisplatin therapy: stop or change the drug.`,`Rapidly progressive bilateral loss over weeks suggests **autoimmune inner ear disease**.`,`Hearing loss is the largest modifiable risk factor for **dementia**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**1-3-6 rule**: screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3, intervene by 6 months.`,`Use ear plugs or muffs above 85 dB(A); **60/60 rule** for personal devices.`,`Vaccinate: measles-rubella, pneumococcal and Hib.`,`**NPPCD** gives screening and free hearing aids; ADIP funds cochlear implants.`,`Screen every adult **aged 50 and over** for age-related hearing loss.`,`RPwD Act: benchmark disability of **40%** unlocks entitlements.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Tuning fork tests (512 Hz)`,columns:[`Type`,`Rinne`,`Weber`,`Absolute bone conduction`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Positive`,`Central`,`Normal`],[`Conductive loss`,`Negative`,`To affected ear`,`Normal`],[`Sensorineural loss`,`Positive, both reduced`,`To better ear`,`Reduced`],[`Dead ear`,`False negative`,`To better ear`,`Reduced; mask with Barany box`],[`Mixed loss`,`Negative`,`Variable`,`Reduced`]]},{heading:`WHO grades of hearing loss (better ear)`,columns:[`Grade`,`Threshold`,`Practical consequence`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Under 20 dB`,`No difficulty`],[`Mild`,`20-34 dB`,`Difficulty with soft speech, noise`],[`Moderate`,`35-49 dB`,`Hearing aid usually needed`],[`Moderately severe`,`50-64 dB`,`Difficulty even with raised voice`],[`Severe`,`65-79 dB`,`Hears only loud speech`],[`Profound`,`80-94 dB`,`Consider cochlear implant`],[`Complete`,`95 dB or more`,`Implant or sign language`]]},{heading:`Drugs in hearing loss`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Prednisolone (SSNHL)`,`1 mg/kg/day oral, max 60 mg, morning`,`7-14 days, then taper`,`Check sugar and BP; screen TB`],[`Dexamethasone intratympanic`,`10-24 mg/mL, 0.4-0.8 mL`,`3-4 injections over 2 weeks`,`Pain, vertigo, perforation`],[`Sodium bicarbonate 5% ear drops`,`3-4 drops 2-3 times daily`,`3-5 days`,`Not with known perforation`],[`Prochlorperazine`,`5-10 mg oral up to TDS`,`Maximum 3 days`,`Drowsiness, dystonia, parkinsonism`],[`Betahistine`,`16 mg TDS or 24 mg BD with food`,`Review at 3-6 months`,`Not in phaeochromocytoma; caution asthma`],[`Valganciclovir (congenital CMV)`,`16 mg/kg oral twice daily`,`6 months`,`Neutropenia; regular counts`]]}],remember:[`Use a **512 Hz fork** - 256 Hz is felt rather than heard.`,`Weber goes **to the conductive ear**, away from the sensorineural ear.`,`Conductive loss never exceeds about 60 dB.`,`A **4 kHz notch** is noise-induced loss.`,`Sudden SNHL is a **stroke of the ear** - steroids within 72 hours.`,`Asymmetric SNHL needs MRI, not just a hearing aid.`,`**1-3-6**: screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3, intervene by 6.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AAO-HNS Age-Related Hearing Loss guideline 2024: screen adults aged 50 and over, refer a positive screen to audiology, fit hearing aids, cochlear implant evaluation when aids give limited benefit`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Hearing loss across ages`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Conductive** (pinna to stapes footplate, maximum about 60 dB) vs **sensorineural** (cochlea or nerve, often irreversible) vs mixed. **SSNHL: 30 dB or more over 3 contiguous frequencies within 72 hours**. Asymmetric SNHL: about 15 dB or more at 2+ frequencies.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**WHO PTA (500-4000 Hz, better ear)**: normal under 20, mild 20-34, moderate 35-49, moderately severe 50-64, severe 65-79, profound 80-94, complete 95+; **disabling over 35 dB**. RPwD Act: deaf 70 dB+, hard of hearing 60-70 dB; benchmark disability 40%.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Conductive: wax, OME, CSOM, otosclerosis. SNHL: **presbycusis, noise (85 dB(A) 8 h), ototoxins** (aminoglycosides, cisplatin, loop diuretics), Meniere, vestibular schwannoma, SSNHL, congenital (**GJB2, congenital CMV**, rubella, prematurity, jaundice, meningitis).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Middle ear transformer (17:1 area, 1.3:1 lever) lost -> conductive loss; **basal hair cells (high frequency) die first** with age, noise, drugs -> consonant loss; outer hair cells amplify (OAE), inner transduce; **4 kHz noise notch**; otosclerosis fixes stapes (**Carhart notch 2 kHz**); hydrops in Meniere.`],[`Key history`,`Onset (sudden = refer today), one or both ears, tinnitus (unilateral = red flag), vertigo, discharge, **noise and drug history** (past TB injections), family history, perinatal risks and **speech milestones**, **parental concern**, neurological symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`Otoscopy after wax removal; free-field voice at 60 cm; **512 Hz fork**: Rinne negative = conductive (15-20 dB+); **Weber to conductive ear, away from SNHL**; ABC reduced in SNHL; dead ear - false negative Rinne (Weber to good ear, Barany box); corneal reflex, VII, cerebellar signs; postnasal space.`],[`Investigations`,`**PTA** (air-bone gap over 10 dB = conductive); speech audiometry (retrocochlear); tympanometry (A, As, Ad, B, C); newborn **OAE + AABR**; BERA/ASSR; **gadolinium MRI IAM** for asymmetric SNHL, unilateral tinnitus, SSNHL; HRCT for cholesteatoma/implant; child SNHL: **CMV PCR within 3 weeks**, GJB2, ECG (JLN).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Treat cause of conductive loss (wax softening **sodium bicarbonate 5% 3-4 drops 2-3x/day 3-5 days**, OME pathway, tympanoplasty, **stapedotomy or aid** in otosclerosis); **hearing aids from moderate loss**, binaural for bilateral; communication tactics - speak clearly, do not shout.`],[`Treatment - SSNHL`,`**Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day (max 60 mg) single morning dose x 7-14 days then taper**, ideally within 72 h (up to 2 weeks); check glucose, BP; **intratympanic dexamethasone** salvage within 2-6 weeks or if systemic steroid unsafe; audiometry and **MRI**; no antivirals or vasodilators.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Cochlear implant** for bilateral severe-profound loss with little aid benefit (congenital by 12-24 months); bone-anchored/CROS aids for discharging ears and single-sided deafness. Meniere: salt 1.5-2 g Na/day, **betahistine 16 mg TDS or 24 mg BD**, vestibular sedative max 3 days, diuretic, intratympanic steroid/gentamicin.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Aid: activate battery 1 min, insert mould, set volume in quiet, **2-4 h/day building over 2-4 weeks**, remove for bath/sleep, dry box; whistling = poor fit or wax; **button batteries away from children**; FM systems in classrooms.`],[`Special groups`,`Newborns: **1-3-6 rule** (screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3, intervene by 6); NICU babies need AABR (auditory neuropathy); symptomatic cCMV - **valganciclovir 16 mg/kg BD x 6 months**; elderly - **screen from 50 (AAO-HNS 2024)**, dementia risk; diabetics on steroids - prefer intratympanic.`],[`Complications`,`Speech and language delay, school failure, social isolation, depression, **dementia (largest modifiable risk factor)**, falls; ototoxic loss progresses after stopping aminoglycosides.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SSNHL (start steroid, refer this week); **asymmetric SNHL or unilateral tinnitus - MRI**; unilateral effusion in adult - postnasal space; loss + vertigo/facial weakness/ataxia - stroke or CPA lesion; head injury with CSF/blood; parent says child does not hear; new tinnitus on aminoglycoside or cisplatin.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Audiometry baseline and during ototoxic therapy; yearly in noise-exposed workers; after SSNHL at end of treatment and 6 months (two-thirds recover partly or fully); **adults 50+ screened, positive to audiology**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NPPCD** (screening, district audiology, free aids), **RBSK** (hearing among 4Ds), **ADIP** (aids, cochlear implants), RPwD certification and UDID; MR, PCV, Hib vaccines; 60/60 rule for earphones; ear plugs above 85 dB(A); all-oral MDR-TB regimens removed injectable ototoxins.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AAO-HNS ARHL 2024**: screen adults 50 and over; refer to audiology; hearing aids; cochlear implant evaluation; asymmetric, conductive, mixed loss or poor word recognition are not simple ageing.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Use 512 Hz fork; Rinne negative = conductive 15-20 dB+; false negative Rinne exposed by Weber; **conductive never over 60 dB**; 4 kHz notch = noise; **SSNHL = stroke of the ear**; asymmetric SNHL needs MRI; OAE misses auditory neuropathy; 1-3-6.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Hearing loss at the bedside - type and urgency`,steps:[{label:`Otoscopy: wax, effusion, perforation?`,detail:`Treat the visible cause first`,tone:`decision`},{label:`512 Hz Rinne and Weber`,detail:`Rinne negative + Weber to same ear = conductive; Weber to other ear = SNHL`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Sudden SNHL within 72 h?`,detail:`Prednisolone 1 mg/kg (max 60 mg); audiometry and MRI; ENT this week`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Asymmetric SNHL or unilateral tinnitus`,detail:`Gadolinium MRI IAM - vestibular schwannoma`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Bilateral symmetric SNHL in older adult`,detail:`Presbycusis or noise; audiology referral; screen all 50+`},{label:`Rehabilitate`,detail:`Hearing aids from 35 dB; implant if severe-profound; NPPCD/ADIP support`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Tuning fork patterns (512 Hz)`,columns:[`Type`,`Rinne (affected ear)`,`Weber`,`ABC`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Positive`,`Central`,`Normal`],[`Conductive`,`Negative`,`To affected ear`,`Normal`],[`Sensorineural`,`Positive (both reduced)`,`To better ear`,`Reduced`],[`Dead ear`,`False negative`,`To better ear`,`Reduced; mask good ear`]]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Newborn hearing - the 1-3-6 rule`,steps:[{label:`Screen by 1 month`,detail:`OAE for well babies; AABR for NICU over 5 days and all high-risk`},{label:`Refer or fail - diagnostic ABR/ASSR by 3 months`,detail:`CMV PCR on urine or saliva within 3 weeks of birth`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Intervene by 6 months`,detail:`Hearing aids, early intervention, speech therapy`},{label:`Severe-profound bilateral loss`,detail:`Cochlear implant by 12-24 months (ADIP)`,tone:`good`},{label:`Passed but high risk`,detail:`Repeat testing - late-onset loss (CMV, meningitis)`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"eye-ent-epistaxis-foreign-bodies":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Epistaxis** is bleeding from the nasal cavity or nasopharynx.`,`About **90% is anterior** from Little's area and stops with pinching.`,`About 5-10% is **posterior**, from sphenopalatine branches, in elderly or anticoagulated patients.`,`**Hypertension prolongs bleeding** but rarely starts it; stop the bleed first.`,`**Button batteries and magnets** are emergencies wherever they lodge.`,`Most foreign bodies occur in children under 5, peaking at **1-3 years**.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Local: nose picking, dry air, rhinitis, septal spur, steroid spray aimed at the septum.`,`Systemic: **anticoagulants and antiplatelets**, thrombocytopenia, liver and kidney disease.`,`Bleeding disorders: haemophilia, von Willebrand disease and **HHT**.`,`Adolescent boy with recurrent unilateral bleeds: **juvenile angiofibroma**.`,`Fever with epistaxis: think **dengue**; check platelets first.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. **Little's area** is where four arteries meet on the anterior-inferior septum.`,`2. They are anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial arteries.`,`3. Dry air causes crusting; picking tears the thin mucosa over the plexus.`,`4. Posterior bleeds run into the pharynx and **cannot be reached by pinching**.`,`5. Septal cartilage depends on its mucosa, so **cautery of both sides perforates it**.`,`6. A button battery makes **hydroxide** at the mucosa, causing necrosis within hours.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Estimate the amount, side, and whether blood runs **down the throat**.`,`Take a full drug history: **warfarin, DOACs, aspirin, clopidogrel**, NSAIDs.`,`Ask about gum bleeding, bruising and menorrhagia for a bleeding disorder.`,`Foreign body: what it was, when, which side, and previous attempts.`,`**Unilateral foul nasal discharge** in a child is a foreign body.`,`A **choking episode** while eating nuts justifies bronchoscopy on its own.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`In heavy bleeding, **resuscitate first**: pulse, BP, postural drop, blood in the pharynx.`,`Blow out clots, insert a vasoconstrictor pledget, then look with headlight and speculum.`,`No anterior point and blood in the pharynx means a **posterior bleed**.`,`Look for telangiectasia, petechiae, liver stigmata and splenomegaly.`,`Always check the **other ear and both nostrils**; children insert more than one.`,`Unilateral wheeze or reduced air entry in a toddler is not asthma.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`A single minor anterior bleed in a healthy patient needs **no tests**.`,`Severe or anticoagulated: Hb, platelets, **INR**, group and save.`,`Suspected angiofibroma: **contrast CT and MRI**, never clinic biopsy.`,`Suspected button battery: X-ray shows a **double-ring halo sign**.`,`Chest X-ray is often normal in inhaled foreign body; strong history means **rigid bronchoscopy**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Sit forward and pinch the soft nose** for 10-15 minutes by the clock.`,`Do not tilt the head back; spit out blood.`,`If pressure fails: **oxymetazoline** or a lidocaine-adrenaline pledget for 5-10 minutes.`,`**Silver nitrate cautery** for 5-10 seconds, one side of the septum only.`,`Then an **anterior nasal tampon** along the floor, removed in 24-48 hours.`,`Do not stop anticoagulants reflexively; local measures usually work.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Tranexamic acid**: topical pledget, oral 1 g three times daily, or IV 1 g.`,`Posterior bleed: **balloon or Foley posterior pack**, admit with oxygen and ECG monitoring.`,`Refractory: **endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation** is first choice.`,`Warfarin with life-threatening bleed: **vitamin K 5 mg IV plus PCC**.`,`Dabigatran is reversed with **idarucizumab 5 g IV**.`,`After cautery: chlorhexidine-neomycin cream; avoid it in peanut allergy.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Button battery in the oesophagus: remove **within 2 hours**.`,`Swallowed battery, child over 1 year: **honey 10 mL every 10 minutes**, up to 6 doses, en route.`,`Choking infant under 1 year: **5 back blows, then 5 chest thrusts**.`,`Choking child over 1 year: 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts.`,`Never do **blind finger sweeps**.`,`Two or more swallowed magnets cause bowel necrosis; urgent removal.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Foreign body: **one or two attempts only**, then refer.`,`Use a **hook behind a round object**; forceps push it deeper.`,`Never syringe a **vegetable foreign body**, a battery, or an ear with a perforation.`,`Refer recurrent **unilateral** epistaxis or obstruction for nasal endoscopy.`,`After a bleed: no nose blowing or straining for **7 days**; petroleum jelly to the septum.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Anterior versus posterior epistaxis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Anterior`,`Posterior`],rows:[[`Proportion`,`About 90%`,`About 5-10%`],[`Source`,`Little's area`,`Sphenopalatine branches, Woodruff's plexus`],[`Typical patient`,`Children, young adults`,`Elderly, hypertensive, anticoagulated`],[`Direction`,`Out of one nostril`,`Into pharynx, swallowed`],[`Pinching`,`Usually works`,`Ineffective`],[`Management`,`Pressure, cautery, anterior pack`,`Posterior pack, admit, SPA ligation`]]},{heading:`Drugs in epistaxis and foreign bodies`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Oxymetazoline 0.05% spray`,`2 sprays into bleeding side`,`Once, before examination`,`BP rise; rebound if used for days`],[`Lidocaine 4% with adrenaline`,`Pledget in the nose`,`5-10 minutes`,`Keep lidocaine under 3 mg/kg`],[`Silver nitrate 75% stick`,`Touch around, then on, the point`,`5-10 seconds, one side`,`Septal perforation if both sides`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`Oral 1 g TDS; IV 1 g; topical 500 mg`,`Oral 5-7 days`,`Avoid in active thrombosis`],[`Chlorhexidine-neomycin cream`,`Inside nostril four times daily`,`10 days`,`Not in peanut allergy`],[`Vitamin K`,`5 mg IV with PCC in major bleed`,`Single dose`,`Give IV slowly`],[`Honey (button battery)`,`10 mL oral every 10 minutes`,`Up to 6 doses within 12 h`,`Only over 1 year; do not delay`]]},{heading:`Foreign bodies by site`,columns:[`Site`,`Key point`,`Action`],rows:[[`Ear`,`Round objects stick at the isthmus`,`Hook behind; no syringing of seeds`],[`Ear, live insect`,`Drown it first`,`Lidocaine or oil, then remove`],[`Nose`,`Unilateral foul discharge`,`Mother's kiss or hook; never push back`],[`Oesophagus`,`Coin lies flat on AP film`,`Endoscopy; battery within 2 hours`],[`Airway`,`Right main bronchus commonest`,`Rigid bronchoscopy even if X-ray normal`]]}],remember:[`**Sit forward, pinch the soft nose**, 10-15 minutes, no peeping.`,`Little's area: anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine, superior labial.`,`Cauterise **one side at a time**.`,`Stop the bleed first, then treat the BP.`,`Never syringe a vegetable, a battery, or a perforated ear.`,`The **first attempt is the best attempt**; refer after two failures.`,`No clinic biopsy in an adolescent boy with recurrent unilateral bleeds.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`ERC 2025 replaces ERC 2021 as the citation for choking first aid; sequence unchanged (back blows then abdominal thrusts over 1 year, chest thrusts in infants, no blind finger sweep)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Epistaxis and ENT foreign bodies`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Epistaxis**: bleeding from nasal cavity or nasopharynx - **anterior (about 90%, Little's area)** vs **posterior (5-10%, sphenopalatine branches, Woodruff plexus)**. **Foreign bodies**: by site (ear, nose, pharynx, oesophagus, airway) and nature (inorganic, organic - swell, animate).`],[`Relevant classification`,`Epistaxis anterior/posterior; local vs systemic causes. Foreign bodies: **button batteries and paired magnets = emergencies anywhere**; airway partial (check-valve, emphysema) vs complete (stop-valve, collapse).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Picking, dry air, rhinitis, misdirected steroid spray, septal spur, **anticoagulants/antiplatelets**, thrombocytopenia (dengue), liver/renal disease, HHT, angiofibroma, tumour; hypertension **prolongs rather than starts** bleeding. FB: **children 1-3 years**, nuts, seeds, beads, batteries.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Little's area = **anterior ethmoidal + sphenopalatine (septal) + greater palatine + superior labial**; dry air -> crust -> picking -> tear. Elderly: rigid vessels + anticoagulant -> posterior heavy bleed. Battery: current -> **hydroxide at negative pole** -> liquefactive necrosis in 15 min, oesophageal perforation within 2 h.`],[`Key history`,`Amount, side, blood **down the throat** (posterior), dizziness, recurrence, **drugs (warfarin, DOAC, aspirin, clopidogrel)**, bleeding elsewhere, fever (dengue), family history (HHT). FB: what, when, which side, previous attempts; **unilateral foul discharge**; choking episode then quiet interval.`],[`Key examination`,`**Resuscitation first** (pulse, BP, postural drop, blood in oropharynx); blow clots, vasoconstrictor pledget, headlight + Thudichum - find point on Little's area; telangiectasia, petechiae. FB: check other ear and both nostrils; drooling, crepitus (oesophagus); **unilateral wheeze or reduced air entry** (bronchus).`],[`Investigations`,`Single minor anterior bleed - **no tests**; severe/recurrent/anticoagulated: Hb, platelets, **INR**, group and save, NS1 in fever. Endoscopy for recurrent/unilateral; **contrast CT/MRI, never clinic biopsy** for angiofibroma. FB: visible ear/nose - no X-ray; **battery - AP + lateral neck, chest, abdomen (halo sign)**; inhaled FB - CXR may be normal, **bronchoscopy on history**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Sit up, lean forward, pinch soft nose 10-15 min by clock**, spit blood, ice; then **oxymetazoline 0.05% or lidocaine 4% + adrenaline pledget 5-10 min**; **silver nitrate 75% cautery 5-10 s, one side only** (other after 4-6 weeks); naseptin (not in peanut allergy) or mupirocin cream.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Anterior **expandable tampon along the floor** (24-48 h) or balloon; posterior: balloon or **Foley pack** + admit, oxygen, ECG; **tranexamic acid** topical 500 mg/5 mL, oral 1 g TDS, IV 1 g; refractory - **endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation** (first choice), anterior ethmoidal ligation, embolisation.`],[`Treatment - anticoagulated patient`,`**Do not stop reflexively** - local measures first; life-threatening warfarin bleed: **vitamin K 5 mg IV + 4-factor PCC 25-50 IU/kg**; dabigatran - **idarucizumab 5 g IV**; Xa inhibitors - PCC 50 IU/kg if no specific reversal; platelets for active bleeding, not a number.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Tampon horizontal along floor, expand with 10 mL saline; **packs out at 24-48 h**; anti-staphylococcal cover for posterior packs or over 48 h (toxic shock). FB: **hook behind round objects** (forceps push deeper), crocodile forceps for soft; **mother's kiss** for nasal FB; syringe only non-vegetable with intact drum.`],[`Special groups`,`Children: retrocolumellar vein, nose picking, nail trimming, petroleum jelly BD x 2 weeks. **Adolescent boy with unilateral bleed + obstruction = angiofibroma**. Elderly anticoagulated - posterior. Dengue - check platelets before instrumentation. HHT - avoid repeated cautery.`],[`Complications`,`Hypovolaemia, aspiration, myocardial ischaemia, **septal perforation** (bilateral cautery), alar necrosis, sinusitis, toxic shock; battery - **aorto-oesophageal or tracheo-oesophageal fistula**, septal perforation; retained bronchial FB - pneumonia, **bronchiectasis**; magnets - bowel necrosis.`],[`Emergency - choking (ERC 2025)`,`Effective cough - encourage; **ineffective, over 1 year: 5 back blows then 5 abdominal thrusts**; **infant: 5 back blows then 5 chest thrusts**; unconscious - CPR, look in mouth, **no blind finger sweep**; stable child - keep upright, rigid bronchoscopy.`],[`Emergency - battery`,`Swallowed within 12 h, over 1 year: **honey 10 mL every 10 min up to 6 doses** en route; hospital **sucralfate 1 g x 3**; **oesophageal removal within 2 hours**; nose/ear battery - remove same hour, no drops or irrigation. **Two magnets** - urgent removal.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Tachycardia, hypotension, blood pouring into pharynx; recurrent unilateral bleed + obstruction (adolescent or adult - tumour); bleed with fever, petechiae; any battery or magnet; drooling, inability to swallow saliva; convincing choking history even with normal X-ray.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`No blowing, straining, hot drinks for **7 days**; pack removal 24-48 h; recheck Hb; review BP and anticoagulant indication; investigate recurrent bleeders (FBC, INR, vWD); after FB removal document side, attempts and drum/septum state.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Humidify, saline gel, **aim steroid sprays away from septum**; no peanuts, whole grapes, hard sweets or popcorn under 4-5 years; secure battery compartments; no eating while running.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ERC 2025** replaces ERC 2021 for choking - sequence unchanged (5 back blows then 5 abdominal thrusts over 1 year, chest thrusts in infants, no blind finger sweep); **AAO-HNS Nosebleed 2020** (do not stop anticoagulants reflexively) remains current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Sit forward, pinch soft nose, 10-15 min, no peeping; **cauterise one side only**; hypertension prolongs not starts epistaxis; never syringe vegetable FB, battery, or perforated ear; **hook behind round objects**; coin in oesophagus lies flat on AP film; battery shows halo.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Epistaxis - stepwise control`,steps:[{label:`First aid: lean forward, pinch soft nose 10-15 min`,detail:`Spit blood, ice pack; resuscitate if shocked`,tone:`good`},{label:`Vasoconstrictor and anaesthetic pledget`,detail:`Oxymetazoline 0.05% or lidocaine 4% + adrenaline 5-10 min; lidocaine under 3 mg/kg`},{label:`Silver nitrate cautery, one side only`,detail:`Around then on the point 5-10 s; antiseptic cream afterwards`},{label:`Anterior pack or balloon`,detail:`Along the floor, remove at 24-48 h; topical tranexamic acid 500 mg`},{label:`Posterior bleed: balloon or Foley pack, admit`,detail:`Oxygen, ECG, IV access, cross-match, antibiotic cover`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Refractory: sphenopalatine artery ligation`,detail:`Anterior ethmoidal ligation or embolisation as alternatives`,tone:`decision`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Choking first aid (ERC 2025)`,steps:[{label:`Is the cough effective?`,detail:`Yes - encourage coughing and watch closely`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Ineffective cough, conscious, over 1 year`,detail:`5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts; repeat`},{label:`Infant under 1 year`,detail:`5 back blows, then 5 chest thrusts; never abdominal thrusts`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Becomes unconscious`,detail:`Start CPR; look in mouth each cycle; no blind finger sweep`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Object out or child stable`,detail:`Hospital review; rigid bronchoscopy if history convincing`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Foreign bodies - the site decides the rule`,columns:[`Site`,`Rule`,`Urgency`],rows:[[`Ear`,`Hook behind round object; drown live insect first; no syringing of seeds`,`Battery same hour; others after 1-2 attempts refer`],[`Nose`,`Mother's kiss or hook; never push back; unilateral foul discharge = FB`,`Battery same hour`],[`Oesophagus`,`AP + lateral X-ray; honey en route for battery`,`Battery within 2 h; food bolus 6-24 h`],[`Airway`,`Back blows and thrusts; keep stable child upright`,`Rigid bronchoscopy on history`]]}]},"eye-ent-eyelid-lacrimal-disorders":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **stye (hordeolum)** is an acute S. aureus abscess of a lid gland.`,`A **chalazion** is a chronic sterile lipogranuloma of a blocked meibomian gland.`,`**Pain is the best discriminator**: stye hurts, chalazion does not.`,`**Blepharitis** is chronic lid-margin inflammation; it is controlled, not cured.`,`**Dry eye** is a symptomatic disease of an unstable, hyperosmolar tear film.`,`**Dacryocystitis** is infection of the lacrimal sac behind a blocked nasolacrimal duct.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**External stye** points at the lash line; **internal stye** points on the conjunctival side.`,`Blepharitis is **anterior** (lash base) or **posterior** (meibomian gland dysfunction).`,`Dry eye is **aqueous-deficient** or **evaporative**; MGD is the commonest cause.`,`**Chandler**: preseptal, orbital cellulitis, subperiosteal abscess, orbital abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis.`,`Lid malpositions: **entropion**, ectropion, trichiasis and ptosis.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. **Meibomian glands** make the tear lipid layer that slows evaporation.`,`2. Blocked ducts and thick meibum cause **meibomian gland dysfunction**.`,`3. Bacterial lipases split meibum into irritant fatty acids.`,`4. Tear **hyperosmolarity** triggers surface inflammation and goblet cell loss.`,`5. This **vicious circle** keeps dry eye going; lubricants and anti-inflammatories break it.`,`6. A persistent membrane at the **valve of Hasner** causes congenital duct block.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Acute painful lump for days is a stye; painless lump for weeks is a chalazion.`,`Ask about **blurred vision, double vision and pain on eye movement**.`,`Dry eye: burning, grittiness, worse in evening and with screens, **paradoxical watering**.`,`Ask about drying drugs: antihistamines, antidepressants, **isotretinoin**, preserved glaucoma drops.`,`Dry mouth and joint pain suggest **Sjogren syndrome**.`,`Ptosis worse in the evening suggests **myasthenia**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check **acuity and pupils** in every swollen eye.`,`Proptosis, painful restricted movement, low vision or RAPD mean **orbital cellulitis**.`,`Express meibomian glands: **toothpaste-like** secretion means MGD.`,`Cylindrical sleeves on the lashes mean **Demodex**.`,`Pressure over the sac with **regurgitation of mucopus (ROPLAS)** means chronic dacryocystitis.`,`Lash loss, ulceration or margin distortion suggests **malignancy**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Stye, chalazion and blepharitis are **clinical diagnoses**.`,`Blood sugar for recurrent styes, dacryocystitis or suspected mucormycosis.`,`**Lacrimal syringing**: regurgitation through the upper punctum means duct block.`,`**Schirmer test under 5 mm** in 5 minutes means aqueous deficiency.`,`**Contrast CT** of orbits and sinuses for suspected orbital cellulitis.`,`Biopsy every **recurrent chalazion over 50**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Warm compresses** 5-10 minutes, 3-4 times a day.`,`**Never squeeze a stye**; valveless veins drain to the cavernous sinus.`,`Topical antibiotics are **not needed** for an uncomplicated stye.`,`Chalazion: compress and massage twice daily for **4-6 weeks**.`,`Blepharitis: compress, massage, then **diluted baby shampoo scrub** twice daily.`,`Dry eye: **carboxymethylcellulose 0.5%** 4-6 times daily; preservative-free if over 4 times.`,`Congenital duct block: **Crigler massage**, 10 strokes four times a day.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Stye spreading as preseptal cellulitis: **oral co-amoxiclav** 625 mg three times daily for 7 days.`,`Chalazion over 6 weeks: **triamcinolone injection** via conjunctiva, or incision and curettage.`,`Moderate MGD or rosacea: **oral doxycycline 100 mg daily** for 6-12 weeks.`,`In pregnancy and under 8 years, use **azithromycin** instead of doxycycline.`,`Severe dry eye (ophthalmologist): **ciclosporin 0.05%** for at least 3-6 months.`,`Acute dacryocystitis: oral co-amoxiclav; **no syringing or probing** in the acute phase.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Orbital cellulitis: admit for **IV ceftriaxone** and contrast CT.`,`Black eschar on nose or palate in a diabetic: **rhino-orbital mucormycosis**.`,`Recurrent chalazion in the elderly: **sebaceous gland carcinoma** until biopsy.`,`Ptosis with a dilated pupil: **third nerve palsy**; emergency CT angiography.`,`Infant watering eye with photophobia and large cornea: **congenital glaucoma**.`,`Never give steroid drops without slit-lamp examination.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review preseptal cellulitis at **24-48 hours**; admit under 1 year or unwell.`,`Congenital duct block: over 90% resolve; **probe at about 12 months** if watering persists.`,`Chronic dacryocystitis needs **DCR**, and treatment before any cataract surgery.`,`Review dry eye and blepharitis at **4-6 weeks**.`,`Screen users: **20-20-20 rule** and screen top at or below eye level.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Lid swellings at the bedside`,columns:[`Type`,`Pain`,`Key sign`,`First-line care`],rows:[[`External stye`,`Painful`,`Points at lash line`,`Warm compress, epilate lash`],[`Internal stye`,`Very painful`,`Points on conjunctival side`,`Compress; antibiotic only if cellulitis`],[`Chalazion`,`Painless`,`Firm nodule away from margin`,`Compress 4-6 weeks, then injection`],[`Acute dacryocystitis`,`Very tender`,`Swelling below medial canthus`,`Oral co-amoxiclav; later DCR`],[`Sebaceous gland carcinoma`,`Painless`,`Recurrent chalazion, lash loss, elderly`,`Urgent biopsy`]]},{heading:`Chandler stages of orbital infection`,columns:[`Stage`,`Name`,`Key finding`,`Management`],rows:[[`I`,`Preseptal cellulitis`,`Normal vision and eye movement`,`Oral co-amoxiclav, review 24-48 h`],[`II`,`Orbital cellulitis`,`Proptosis, painful restricted movement`,`Admit, IV antibiotics, CT`],[`III`,`Subperiosteal abscess`,`Globe displaced away`,`IV antibiotics; drain if large`],[`IV`,`Orbital abscess`,`Ophthalmoplegia, vision loss`,`Urgent drainage`],[`V`,`Cavernous sinus thrombosis`,`Bilateral signs, cranial nerve palsies`,`ICU, IV antibiotics`]]},{heading:`Drugs in lid and lacrimal disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Chloramphenicol 1% ointment`,`Twice daily, lid margin at night`,`5-7 days; blepharitis 2-4 weeks`,`Stinging, blur`],[`Co-amoxiclav`,`Adult 625 mg oral TDS`,`7-10 days`,`Diarrhoea, rash; take with food`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral once daily`,`6-12 weeks`,`Photosensitivity; not in pregnancy or under 8`],[`Azithromycin 1% eye drops`,`Lid margin BD 2 days, then daily`,`2-4 weeks`,`Irritation, blur`],[`Carboxymethylcellulose 0.5%`,`1 drop 4-6 times daily`,`Long term`,`Preservative-free if over 4 times daily`],[`Ciclosporin 0.05% emulsion`,`1 drop twice daily`,`At least 3-6 months`,`Burning on instillation`],[`Triamcinolone 40 mg/mL`,`0.1-0.2 mL intralesional`,`Once; repeat after 2-4 weeks`,`Skin depigmentation; inject via conjunctiva`],[`Ceftriaxone (orbital cellulitis)`,`Adult 2 g IV twice daily`,`Inpatient course`,`Add metronidazole or vancomycin as needed`]]}],remember:[`Stye is **painful at the lash line**; chalazion is painless and away from the margin.`,`Chalazion incision is **vertical on the conjunctival side**.`,`Recurrent chalazion in the elderly - **biopsy** for sebaceous carcinoma.`,`**ROPLAS** means duct block - fix it before cataract surgery.`,`Any of proptosis, painful ophthalmoplegia, low vision or RAPD means orbital cellulitis.`,`Doxycycline works on **lipase**, not bacteria, in blepharitis.`,`Preservative-free drops if needed **more than four times a day**.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`TFOS DEWS III 2025: dry eye redefined as a symptomatic, multifactorial disease with loss of homeostasis of the tear film and/or ocular surface`,`TFOS DEWS III 2025: diagnosis = OSDI-6 score 4 or more plus one sign (non-invasive break-up time under 10 s, osmolarity 308 mOsm/L or more or inter-eye difference over 8, or ocular surface staining) after excluding mimics`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Eyelid and lacrimal disorders, dry eye`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Stye (hordeolum)**: acute S. aureus abscess - external (Zeis/Moll, lash line) or internal (meibomian). **Chalazion**: chronic **sterile lipogranuloma** of a blocked meibomian gland - **painless**. **Blepharitis**: chronic lid-margin inflammation. **Dry eye (TFOS DEWS III)**: **symptomatic** loss of tear film and/or ocular surface homeostasis. **Dacryocystitis**: infected sac behind NLDO.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Blepharitis **anterior** (staphylococcal, seborrhoeic, Demodex) vs **posterior (MGD)**; dry eye **aqueous-deficient vs evaporative** (MGD commonest); NLDO congenital/acquired; **Chandler I-V** orbital infection; lid malpositions (entropion, ectropion, trichiasis, ptosis).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Seborrhoea, **rosacea**, Demodex, diabetes, refractive error, screens (**blink rate 15 -> 5/min**), AC, contact lenses, drugs (antihistamines, antidepressants, beta-blockers, isotretinoin, **preserved glaucoma drops**), Sjogren, trachoma/SJS scarring, facial palsy.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`MGD: duct keratinisation -> thick meibum -> **bacterial lipases -> free fatty acids** -> unstable film -> **hyperosmolarity -> IL-1, TNF, MMP-9** -> goblet loss -> vicious circle. Chalazion: lipid leak -> granuloma. NLDO: Hasner valve (congenital) or fibrosis -> stasis -> infection (pneumococcal reservoir -> post-cataract endophthalmitis).`],[`Key history`,`Lump: pain and duration (painful days = stye; painless weeks = chalazion); **vision, diplopia, painful eye movement, fever** (orbital); burning, grittiness, morning crusts, **paradoxical watering**, screen hours; dry mouth (Sjogren); watering + sticky discharge; infant watering + photophobia (glaucoma); fatigable ptosis.`],[`Key examination`,`**Acuity and RAPD in every swollen eye**; eye movements and proptosis; palpate lump, evert lid; lid margin (collarettes, cylindrical sleeves, capped orifices); **express meibomian glands**; fluorescein staining, **NIBUT**; **ROPLAS** on sac pressure; lid position, lagophthalmos, Bell; levator function; lesions with lash loss.`],[`Investigations`,`Stye/chalazion/blepharitis - **clinical**. Dry eye (DEWS III): **OSDI-6 4+ plus one sign** - NIBUT under 10 s, osmolarity 308+ (or difference over 8), or staining; **Schirmer under 5 mm/5 min** = aqueous deficiency; anti-Ro/La if Sjogren; **lacrimal syringing** before cataract surgery; contrast CT orbit; **biopsy recurrent chalazion over 50**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Warm compress 5-10 min 3-4x/day**; never squeeze a stye, epilate the lash; chalazion compress + massage **4-6 weeks**; **lid hygiene** (diluted baby shampoo 1:10) BD then daily - lifelong; dry eye: **preservative-free carboxymethylcellulose 0.5% or hyaluronate 0.1% 4-6x/day**, gel at night; 20-20-20 rule.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Chalazion: **triamcinolone 40 mg/mL 0.1-0.2 mL** via conjunctiva or **vertical incision and curettage**. Blepharitis: azithromycin 1% drops or chloramphenicol ointment to margin; **doxycycline 100 mg OD 6-12 weeks** (anti-lipase/MMP); Demodex - tea tree oil, ivermectin, lotilaner. Dry eye: **ciclosporin 0.05% BD 3-6 months**, loteprednol pulse, punctal plugs.`],[`Treatment - infections`,`Stye spreading as **preseptal cellulitis: co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS x 7 days**, review 24-48 h; acute dacryocystitis - co-amoxiclav, **no probing in acute phase**, later **DCR**; orbital cellulitis - **admit, IV ceftriaxone** + vancomycin/metronidazole, CT; mucormycosis - liposomal amphotericin 5 mg/kg/day + debridement.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`One drop in lower lid pocket, close eye 1-2 min, **punctal occlusion**, 5 min between drops, ointment last (1 cm ribbon, bedtime); preservative-free if over 4 times a day; discard bottle 4 weeks after opening; lid-margin ointment rubbed into lash line.`],[`Special groups`,`**Congenital NLDO: Crigler massage 10 strokes QID**, antibiotic only for pus, **probe at about 12 months** (over 90% resolve by 1 year); preseptal cellulitis **under 1 year - admit**; doxycycline not in pregnancy or under 8 - **azithromycin** instead; triamcinolone via conjunctiva in dark skin (depigmentation).`],[`Complications`,`Preseptal -> orbital cellulitis -> abscess -> **cavernous sinus thrombosis**; chalazion astigmatism; corneal ulcer from trichiasis/exposure; post-cataract endophthalmitis from untreated dacryocystitis; **missed sebaceous gland carcinoma**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Proptosis, painful restricted movement, reduced vision, RAPD - admit; black eschar in diabetic; **recurrent chalazion same site or unilateral chronic blepharitis over 50 - biopsy**; lash loss, ulceration, bleeding; ptosis + dilated pupil (aneurysm); infant watering + large hazy cornea (congenital glaucoma).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Preseptal cellulitis 24-48 h; chalazion not resolved at 6 weeks - refer; dry eye and blepharitis 4-6 weeks for adherence; **lacrimal syringing before every cataract referral**; NPCBVI school screening for children with screen strain.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**TFOS DEWS III 2025**: dry eye is always symptomatic; tear film and/or ocular surface; diagnosis by OSDI-6 4+ plus one sign (NIBUT under 10 s, osmolarity, staining) after excluding mimics; subtype the drivers.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Stye painful at lash line, chalazion painless away from margin; **vertical conjunctival incision** for chalazion; recurrent chalazion in elderly = sebaceous carcinoma until histology; ROPLAS = NLDO; probe at 12 months; doxycycline works on lipase not bacteria; blepharitis is controlled, not cured.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Swollen eyelid - lid problem or orbit?`,steps:[{label:`Acuity, pupils, eye movements, proptosis`,detail:`Any abnormality = orbital cellulitis; admit, IV ceftriaxone, CT`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Painful lump at lash line`,detail:`External stye - warm compress, epilate lash, never squeeze`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Painless firm nodule away from margin`,detail:`Chalazion - compress and massage 4-6 weeks`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Spreading lid redness, normal eye`,detail:`Preseptal cellulitis - co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS 7 days; admit if under 1 year`},{label:`Recurrent at same site, age over 50`,detail:`Biopsy - sebaceous gland carcinoma`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Persistent chalazion`,detail:`Triamcinolone 0.1-0.2 mL or incision and curettage`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dry eye - diagnose and step up (TFOS DEWS III)`,steps:[{label:`Exclude mimics`,detail:`Allergy, infection, lid malposition, drug toxicity`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Symptom screen: OSDI-6 score 4 or more`,detail:`Grittiness, burning, fluctuating vision, paradoxical watering`},{label:`One sign confirms`,detail:`NIBUT under 10 s, osmolarity 308+ or difference over 8, or staining`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Step 1: education, environment, lid hygiene`,detail:`Preservative-free lubricant 4-6x/day; gel at night; 20-20-20 rule`},{label:`Step 2 (ophthalmologist)`,detail:`Ciclosporin 0.05% BD 3-6 months, loteprednol pulse, plugs, doxycycline for MGD`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Lid lumps compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Stye`,`Chalazion`,`Sebaceous carcinoma`],rows:[[`Pain`,`Painful`,`Painless`,`Painless`],[`Site`,`Lash line or conjunctival point`,`Away from margin, firm`,`Recurrent at same site, lash loss`],[`Patient`,`Any age`,`Any age`,`Elderly`],[`Action`,`Warm compress, epilate lash`,`Compress, then steroid or I and C`,`Biopsy, urgent referral`]]}]},"eye-ent-corneal-ulcer-ocular-trauma":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Infective keratitis** is a fluorescein-staining epithelial defect over a white stromal infiltrate.`,`It causes pain, photophobia, reduced vision and **ciliary congestion**.`,`In South India **fungal keratitis is 40-50%** of culture-positive ulcers.`,`Classic history: **paddy leaf, sugarcane or twig** injury at harvest.`,`Corneal opacity is the **second commonest cause of blindness** over 50 in India.`,`Eye injuries peak at harvest, **Diwali** and Holi; lime burns are typically Indian.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Keratitis by organism: **bacterial, fungal, viral (HSV, zoster), Acanthamoeba**.`,`**BETT**: closed globe (contusion, lamellar) versus open globe.`,`Open globe: rupture, penetrating, perforating and **intraocular foreign body**.`,`**Roper-Hall** grades chemical injury by corneal clarity and limbal ischaemia.`,`Hyphaema grades I-IV; grade IV is a total **eight-ball hyphaema**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Most organisms need an **epithelial breach** from trauma, lens wear or dry eye.`,`2. Bacterial enzymes digest stroma; Pseudomonas can **perforate within 48 hours**.`,`3. Fungi grow slowly along lamellae, giving **feathery margins and satellite lesions**.`,`4. HSV reactivates from the trigeminal ganglion to form a **dendritic ulcer**.`,`5. Steroids suppress defence, spread fungus and turn a dendrite **geographic**.`,`6. **Alkali saponifies** membranes and penetrates deeply; acid coagulates and limits itself.`,`7. Loss of **limbal stem cells** decides the prognosis in chemical injury.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`In a chemical splash, **irrigate before taking any history**.`,`Ask the mechanism: vegetative matter, **hammering metal on metal**, chemical, blunt object.`,`Ask about **contact lens wear**, overnight use and tap water.`,`Ask what was put in the eye: **OTC steroid drops**, anaesthetic drops, herbal juices.`,`Progression in hours suggests bacteria; days suggests fungus; recurrent suggests herpes.`,`Ask about **tetanus immunisation** after any penetrating injury.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **visual acuity first**; it is the medicolegal baseline.`,`**Fluorescein with cobalt-blue light**: dendrite with terminal bulbs means HSV.`,`**Multiple vertical linear abrasions** mean a subtarsal foreign body; evert the lid.`,`Test **corneal sensation** before anaesthetic; reduced sensation suggests HSV.`,`Open globe signs: **peaked pupil**, uveal prolapse, positive Seidel test, hypotony.`,`If open globe is suspected, **stop examining**; no pressure on the eye.`,`Diplopia on upgaze with infraorbital numbness suggests **orbital floor fracture**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Fluorescein staining** is the first test in every painful red eye and injury.`,`**Corneal scraping** for Gram stain, KOH and culture before starting treatment.`,`**KOH mount** shows fungal hyphae within minutes.`,`**Litmus paper** after irrigation; continue until pH is 7.0-7.2.`,`X-ray or CT for intraocular foreign body; **never MRI** if iron is possible.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Chemical injury: irrigate **1-2 litres over 15-30 minutes**, sweep the fornices.`,`Open globe: **rigid shield without pressure**, no drops, nil by mouth, antiemetic.`,`Corneal abrasion: **chloramphenicol 1% ointment** four times daily for 3-5 days.`,`Lens wearer abrasion: **moxifloxacin 0.5%** and stop lens wear.`,`Fungal: **natamycin 5% hourly** day and night for 48-72 hours.`,`HSV epithelial: **ganciclovir 0.15% gel** or oral aciclovir; no steroid.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Severe bacterial: **fortified cefazolin plus tobramycin** hourly round the clock.`,`Add **voriconazole 1% drops** for Aspergillus or deep fungal ulcers.`,`Oral voriconazole adds no benefit (**MUTT II**).`,`Recurrent HSV: **oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily** for 12 months.`,`Acanthamoeba: **PHMB or chlorhexidine 0.02%** for 6-12 months.`,`Open globe: **oral moxifloxacin 400 mg** daily and repair within 24 hours.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Untreated ulcers scar as **nebula, macula or leucoma**, or perforate.`,`Pain out of proportion in a lens wearer is **Acanthamoeba**.`,`Hyphaema can **rebleed at 2-5 days**; avoid aspirin and NSAIDs.`,`Angle recession after hyphaema can cause **glaucoma years later**.`,`Penetrating injury can cause **sympathetic ophthalmia** in the other eye.`,`Never send a patient home with **topical anaesthetic drops**; never pad an infected eye.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review an abrasion at **24-48 hours**; any infiltrate means referral.`,`**Same-day referral** for infiltrate, hypopyon, open globe, chemical injury or hyphaema.`,`Tetanus: **Td 0.5 mL IM** if last dose over 5 years; immunoglobulin if unimmunised.`,`Write a **medicolegal note** for assault and workplace injury.`,`Promote **eye donation**; National Eye Donation Fortnight is 25 August-8 September.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of infective keratitis`,columns:[`Type`,`Setting`,`Lesion`,`Test and first drug`],rows:[[`Bacterial`,`Lens wear; hours to 1-2 days`,`Dense infiltrate, mobile sterile hypopyon`,`Gram stain; fortified drops or moxifloxacin`],[`Fungal`,`Vegetative trauma, farmer, steroid misuse`,`Feathery edge, satellites, thick hypopyon`,`KOH mount; natamycin 5%`],[`Herpes simplex`,`Recurrent; reduced sensation`,`Dendrite with terminal bulbs`,`Clinical; ganciclovir gel or aciclovir`],[`Acanthamoeba`,`Lens wear with tap or pool water`,`Pain out of proportion, ring infiltrate`,`Non-nutrient agar; PHMB or chlorhexidine`]]},{heading:`BETT classification of mechanical injury`,columns:[`Type`,`Mechanism`,`Example`],rows:[[`Closed - contusion`,`Blunt force, no wound`,`Cricket ball, fist; hyphaema`],[`Closed - lamellar`,`Partial-thickness wound`,`Abrasion, superficial foreign body`],[`Open - rupture`,`Blunt force at weakest point`,`Bull horn, fall onto a stick`],[`Open - penetrating`,`Sharp object, entry wound only`,`Knife, glass, arrow`],[`Open - perforating`,`Entry and exit wounds`,`Pellet gun`],[`Open - IOFB`,`Retained fragment`,`Hammer and chisel, firecracker`]]},{heading:`Drugs in corneal ulcer and trauma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Natamycin 5% suspension`,`Hourly day and night, then 2-hourly`,`3-4 weeks, 1-2 weeks after healing`,`Irritation; shake well`],[`Fortified cefazolin 5% + tobramycin 1.3%`,`Every 5 min x 5, then hourly`,`24-48 h, then taper`,`Epithelial toxicity; refrigerate`],[`Moxifloxacin 0.5% drops`,`Hourly (ulcer) or QID (abrasion)`,`Taper; abrasion 5 days`,`Stinging, rising resistance`],[`Chloramphenicol 1% ointment`,`Four times daily`,`3-5 days`,`Blur, stinging`],[`Ganciclovir 0.15% gel`,`Five times daily till healed, then TDS`,`Plus 7 days`,`Blur`],[`Aciclovir oral`,`400 mg five times daily`,`7-10 days`,`Reduce in renal impairment`],[`Atropine 1%`,`Twice daily`,`While ulcer or iritis active`,`Near blur, glare`],[`Acetazolamide`,`250 mg oral four times daily`,`Short term`,`Avoid in sickle cell, sulpha allergy`],[`Oral moxifloxacin (open globe)`,`400 mg once daily`,`Until repair`,`QT prolongation, tendinopathy`]]}],remember:[`Feathery margins and satellites after vegetative trauma - **fungus; do KOH**.`,`**Natamycin** is first-line for filamentous fungal keratitis (MUTT I).`,`Dendrite with reduced sensation is HSV - **no steroid**.`,`Lens wearer abrasion needs **moxifloxacin**, not chloramphenicol.`,`Chemical injury: **irrigate first**, until pH 7.0-7.2; alkali is worse than acid.`,`Open globe: **shield without pressure**, no drops, nil by mouth.`,`Iron foreign body possible - **never MRI**.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Corneal ulcer and ocular trauma`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Infective keratitis**: epithelial defect that **stains with fluorescein over a white stromal infiltrate**, with pain, photophobia, reduced vision, ciliary flush. **Ocular trauma**: mechanical (closed or open globe), chemical, thermal.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By organism - **bacterial, fungal (Fusarium, Aspergillus), HSV, Acanthamoeba**. **BETT**: closed globe (contusion, lamellar) vs open globe (rupture, penetrating, perforating, IOFB). **Roper-Hall I-IV** chemical injury by corneal haze and **limbal ischaemia**. Hyphaema grades I-IV.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Vegetative trauma at harvest** (fungal 40-50% of culture-positive ulcers in South India); **contact lens** wear, tap water (Pseudomonas, Acanthamoeba); OTC steroid drops, home remedies, topical anaesthetic abuse; diabetes, dry eye, exposure; **lime (chuna) alkali**, firecrackers, hammering metal.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Epithelial breach -> invasion (gonococcus, diphtheria, Listeria penetrate intact epithelium) -> **Pseudomonas proteases + MMPs melt stroma** in 48 h; fungus grows along lamellae (feathery edge, satellites, endothelial plaque); HSV latent in trigeminal ganglion; **alkali saponifies and penetrates**, acid coagulates; limbal stem cell loss -> conjunctivalisation.`],[`Key history`,`Time, place, **mechanism** (vegetative, metal on metal, chemical type); **irrigate chemical before history**; contact lens habits; drops and remedies used; speed (hours = bacterial, days = fungal, recurrent = HSV, weeks + severe pain = Acanthamoeba); cold sores, zoster rash; tetanus status; medicolegal details.`],[`Key examination`,`**Acuity first** (medicolegal baseline); infiltrate size, hypopyon height; **fluorescein** (dendrite with terminal bulbs; vertical linear scratches = subtarsal FB); evert/double-evert lids; **corneal sensation** before anaesthetic; **open globe signs** - peaked pupil, uveal prolapse, **Seidel positive**, hypotony - stop and shield; RAPD; upgaze diplopia, infraorbital numbness.`],[`Investigations`,`**Corneal scraping before treatment**: Gram stain, **10% KOH** (hyphae in minutes), culture (blood, chocolate, Sabouraud; non-nutrient agar + E. coli for Acanthamoeba); **litmus pH until 7.0-7.2**; **X-ray or CT for IOFB - never MRI**; B-scan if no open globe; glucose, HIV in severe ulcers.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Abrasion: **chloramphenicol 1% ointment QID 3-5 days**; lens wearer - **moxifloxacin 0.5%**, stop lenses. **Fungal: natamycin 5% hourly day and night 48-72 h, then 2-hourly, 3-4 weeks** (MUTT I). Bacterial severe: **fortified cefazolin 5% + tobramycin 1.3%**, loading q5 min x 5 then hourly; small peripheral - moxifloxacin hourly.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Add **topical voriconazole 1%** for Aspergillus/deep ulcers - **oral voriconazole adds nothing (MUTT II)**; HSV - **ganciclovir 0.15% gel 5x/day** or oral aciclovir 400 mg 5x/day; recurrent HSV aciclovir 400 mg BD 12 months; zoster - aciclovir 800 mg 5x/day 7 days; Acanthamoeba - PHMB/chlorhexidine 0.02% for 6-12 months; glue or therapeutic keratoplasty for perforation.`],[`Treatment - adjuncts`,`**Cycloplegic** (atropine 1% BD or homatropine 2% TDS); timolol/acetazolamide for raised IOP (**no acetazolamide in sickle cell**); **adjunct steroid only by specialist after 48 h of effective antibiotic - SCUT no overall benefit; never in fungal or HSV epithelial**; paracetamol analgesia.`],[`Treatment - chemical injury`,`**Irrigate 1-2 litres over 15-30 min** (any clean water first), topical anaesthetic, speculum, sweep fornices, **pH 7.0-7.2**; then preservative-free antibiotic, cycloplegic, **prednisolone 1% for first 7-10 days only**, **ascorbate 10% drops + oral vitamin C 1-2 g + doxycycline 100 mg BD**; amniotic membrane graft in severe burns.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Hourly drops with alarm, night doses essential first 48 h; **5 min between drops**, shake natamycin; **fortified drops refrigerated, discard after 7 days**; **rigid shield on bony rim, never on the globe**; irrigation via IV set; clinic kit - fluorescein, anaesthetic, litmus, shield, cobalt-blue torch.`],[`Emergency - open globe`,`**Shield without pressure, no drops/ointment, nil by mouth**, antiemetic, analgesia, **oral moxifloxacin 400 mg OD** or IV vancomycin + ceftazidime, **Td 0.5 mL (+ TIG 250 IU if unimmunised)**, repair within 24 h; warn about sympathetic ophthalmia.`],[`Special groups`,`Farmers - examine any harvest injury within 24 h; remote setting - **natamycin + moxifloxacin + atropine** after KOH and refer within 24 h; hyphaema in **sickle cell** - urgent; children - atropine toxicity; contact lens wearers - Pseudomonas and Acanthamoeba.`],[`Complications`,`Perforation, **endophthalmitis**, corneal scar (nebula, macula, leucoma, adherent leucoma), staphyloma, **limbal stem cell failure**, symblepharon, secondary glaucoma, angle recession glaucoma, rebleed at 2-5 days in hyphaema, **sympathetic ophthalmia**, siderosis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Any infiltrate or hypopyon - scrape and refer same day**; painful lens-wearer eye; chemical injury after irrigation; open globe/IOFB; hyphaema with raised IOP or sickle cell; **orbital compartment syndrome - lateral canthotomy within 90 min**; upgaze diplopia + enophthalmos (blow-out); new RAPD.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Abrasion 24-48 h; ulcer daily - infiltrate size, epithelial defect, hypopyon, vision; worsening - rescrape; post-hyphaema gonioscopy (angle recession); keratoplasty assessment for scars; medicolegal note.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Protective eyewear (farm, industry, welding), safe Diwali, lens hygiene (no sleeping, swimming or tap water); **no OTC steroid drops or home remedies**; **eye donation fortnight 25 August-8 September**; corneal opacity is the 2nd commonest cause of blindness at 50+ (NBVIS).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No new guideline to 2026: **AAO PPP Bacterial Keratitis 2023**; trial answers stand - **MUTT I (natamycin beats voriconazole for Fusarium)**, **MUTT II (oral voriconazole adds nothing)**, **SCUT (adjunct steroid no overall benefit)**, HEDS (aciclovir 400 mg BD halves HSV recurrence).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Feathery margins + satellites after vegetative trauma = fungus, do KOH; **natamycin first-line (MUTT I)**; dendrite + reduced sensation = HSV, no steroid; pain out of proportion in lens wearer = Acanthamoeba; **irrigate before anything** in chemical injury; never MRI for metallic FB; never send home anaesthetic drops.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Corneal ulcer - diagnose before you treat`,steps:[{label:`Painful red eye with white infiltrate`,detail:`Fluorescein stains defect; record acuity and hypopyon height`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Stop steroids, remedies, anaesthetic drops`,detail:`Never pad an infected eye`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Scrape before first drop`,detail:`Gram stain, 10% KOH, culture; non-nutrient agar if lens wearer`},{label:`Hyphae on KOH: natamycin 5% hourly`,detail:`Add topical voriconazole for deep or Aspergillus; no oral voriconazole`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Bacterial: fortified cefazolin + tobramycin`,detail:`q5 min x 5 then hourly 24-48 h; small peripheral - moxifloxacin`},{label:`Daily review, taper by response`,detail:`Worsening at 48-72 h - rescrape, cornea specialist`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Chemical injury - first hour`,steps:[{label:`Irrigate immediately with any clean water`,detail:`Before history; alkali (lime, cement) worse than acid`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Topical anaesthetic, speculum, saline or RL`,detail:`1-2 litres over 15-30 min; double-evert lids, sweep fornices`},{label:`Check pH after 5-10 min pause`,detail:`Continue until 7.0-7.2`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Grade by limbal ischaemia (Roper-Hall)`,detail:`Over half limbal ischaemia = grade IV, poor prognosis`},{label:`Same-day ophthalmology`,detail:`Steroid first 7-10 days, ascorbate, doxycycline, cycloplegic`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Infective keratitis - telling the organisms apart`,columns:[`Type`,`Setting`,`Lesion`,`First drug`],rows:[[`Bacterial`,`Contact lens, hours`,`Dense infiltrate, mobile sterile hypopyon`,`Fortified cefazolin + tobramycin or moxifloxacin`],[`Fungal`,`Vegetative trauma, days`,`Dry slough, feathery edge, satellites, immobile hypopyon`,`Natamycin 5%`],[`Herpes simplex`,`Recurrent, reduced sensation`,`Dendrite with terminal bulbs`,`Ganciclovir gel or oral aciclovir`],[`Acanthamoeba`,`Lens + tap water, weeks`,`Pain out of proportion, ring infiltrate`,`PHMB or chlorhexidine 0.02%`]]}]}};export{e as default};